s is the single highest-value change here.\n\n---\n\n## Changes\n\n### 1. `workshop_infrastructure/utils.py` \u2014 one line\n\nIn `load_pretrained_weights`, add `head_{k}` to the candidate list:\n\n```python\nfor candidate in (k, f\"backbone.{k}\", f\"head_{k}\"):\n```\n\n- Safe and general: the existing `v.shape == model_state[candidate].shape` guard means it only\n  fires when the shapes actually agree. With `ft_out_chans=1` the decoder is skipped\n  automatically; the 1D model has no `head_unembed.unembed.*` so nothing changes there.\n- Update the docstring to mention the `head_` spelling (it currently documents only two).\n- Expected result: **`Loaded 159 / 159`**.\n\n### 2. New file `downstream_apps/flare_forecast/lightning_modules/pl_flare_2d.py`\n\n`FlareLightningModule` (`lightning_modules/pl_simple_baseline.py`) builds its target as\n`batch[\"forecast\"].unsqueeze(1).float()`. That is right for a scalar `(B,)` label but for a 2D\ntarget it gives `(B, 1, 13, 1, 4096, 4096)`; the metric then compares 33.5M elements against\n436M \u2192 `RuntimeError` on shape mismatch. So the target construction is the *only* thing that\nneeds to change.\n\n- Subclass `FlareLightningModule` and override just the target extraction, so all the\n  logging/optimizer/metric-combination logic is reused rather than copied. Cleanest shape:\n  factor the one line in the parent into a `_target(batch)` method and override it in the\n  child \u2014 a small edit to `pl_simple_baseline.py` that leaves its behaviour identical.\n- The 2D target is `batch[\"forecast\"][:, :, 0].float()` \u2192 `(B, 13, 4096, 4096)`, matching the\n  decoder output.\n- **Assert** `batch[\"forecast\"].shape[2] == 1` with a message pointing at `rollout_steps`:\n  dropping axis 2 is only correct because `rollout_steps: 0` yields exactly one target frame.\n  Silent wrong-axis indexing is the classic failure here.\n\nDo **not** edit `pl_simple_baseline.py`'s behaviour \u2014 `1_baseline_template.ipynb` still needs\nthe scalar path.\n\n### 3. Metrics \u2014 no new file\n\n`metrics/flare_metrics.py` already flattens both arguments with `reshape(-1)`, so MSE and RRSE\nwork unchanged on images. Reuse `FlareMetrics` as-is.\n- Caveat to mention in a markdown cell: RRSE over 436M elements per step is slow. If training\n  drags, drop `train_metrics` and keep the diagnostics in `val_metrics` only.\n\n### 4. Notebook edits, by current cell index\n\n| Cell | Action |\n|---|---|\n| **13** | **Uncomment.** This is the shape check that shows `forecast` is `(B, 13, 1, 4096, 4096)`. Keep the batch in a variable \u2014 cells 28/30/32 need it. |\n| **17** | **The fix.** Drop `num_outputs=1`; add `ft_out_chans=len(cfg.data.channels)` and `ft_unembedding_type=\"linear\"`. Print the expected output shape. |\n| **19** | No code change, but the printed count is now the check: expect `159 / 159`. Add a markdown note that the head is pretrained, not random. |\n| **21** | No change needed to make it run. See \"LoRA note\" below. |\n| **25** | Change `from downstream_apps.template.metrics.template_metrics import FlareMetrics` \u2192 `from downstream_apps.flare_forecast.metrics.flare_metrics import FlareMetrics` (this app's own copy). |\n| **28 / 30 / 32** | Rewrite to use the 2D target: `target = batch[\"forecast\"][:, :, 0].float()` and a real `output` from cell 23. Currently they reference an undefined `output` and the raw 5D `batch[\"forecast\"]`. |\n| **34** | Change to `from downstream_apps.flare_forecast.lightning_modules.pl_flare_2d import Flare2DLightningModule`. |\n| **38** | Swap the class name; arguments unchanged. |\n| **NEW, after 21** | **Prediction plot #1** (before training) \u2014 see below. |\n| **NEW, after 44** | **Prediction plot #2** (after training), same helper, so the two are directly comparable. |\n\n### 5. The plotting cell (what you actually asked for)\n\nReuse the plotting helpers already written in `0_dataset_TEST-2.ipynb` **cell 21** (`_lim()` and\n`_show()`, with `sunpy.visualization.colormaps` registering `sdoaia171` / `hmimag`). Wrap them\nin a function `plot_prediction(model, loader, channel=\"aia171\", ...)` in the notebook that:\n\n- takes one batch, runs `model.eval()` + `torch.no_grad()` on a **batch of 1** (a 13\u00d74096\u00d74096\n  fp32 prediction is ~870 MB, so don't plot the whole batch);\n- converts to physical units with `loader.dataset.inverse_transform_data(arr)` where `arr` is\n  `(13, H, W)` float32 numpy. Per the \"THREE SPACES\" block at the top of\n  `workshop_infrastructure/datasets/helio.py`, the model works in **normalized** space, and\n  `inverse_transform_data` undoes *both* the z-score and the signum-log \u2192 DN / Gauss. This is\n  the reason to keep `ft_out_chans=13`: `inverse_transform_data` asserts `ndim == 3` and indexes\n  per-channel stats, so a 1-channel output cannot use it without slicing the scalers;\n- lays out one row per plotted channel, 4 columns: **input t\u221260 \u00b7 input t\u221236 (now) \u00b7\n  ground truth t+0 \u00b7 prediction**, plus a 5th `prediction \u2212 ground truth` difference panel on\n  `coolwarm`. The difference panel is what actually tells you whether it is working;\n- titles each panel with the real timestamp, derived the way `0_dataset_TEST-2.ipynb` cell 21\n  does it (`now_time` from the last sorted input offset, then `item[\"lead_time_delta\"]`).\n\n---\n\n## Notes worth acting on, but not required to make it run\n\n- **LoRA `target_modules` is silently wrong.** The YAML has\n  `[q_proj, v_proj, k_proj, out_proj, fc1, fc2]`. Grepping `workshop_infrastructure/models/`\n  finds **no** `q_proj`/`k_proj`/`v_proj`/`out_proj` \u2014 those are HuggingFace names, not Surya's.\n  PEFT only raises when *nothing* matches, and `fc1`/`fc2` do, so the four bogus names are\n  dropped without a word and **attention is never adapted**. Per `CLAUDE.md` the correct list is\n  `[fc1, fc2, attn.qkv, attn.proj]`. Verify against the `[LoRA] Adapted modules` list printed at\n  startup. Recommend fixing this in the same pass.\n- **`flip_probability` does nothing today.** `build_helio_dataloaders` doesn't forward it, so it\n  defaults to 0.0. Also note what it does when you do enable it: `flare_dataset.py:147-153`\n  flips `ts` **and** `forecast` together, which is plain augmentation \u2014 it does not create an\n  \"un-flip\" task. If the intent is \"flip the input, keep the target upright\", flip only `ts`.\n  Left as a follow-up; the basic example doesn't need it.\n- **`ground_truth`** (`flare_dataset.py:142`) is still the scalar flare intensity. Harmless on\n  the 2D path (nothing reads it) and useful as a plot annotation. Leaving it.\n\n### If it OOMs (GPU here is an L40S, 46 GB \u2014 not an 80 GB A100)\n\nIn order, cheapest first: `training.batch_size: 1` \u2192 `ft_out_chans: 1` with the target sliced\nto one channel (loses the pretrained decoder and `inverse_transform_data`) \u2192 `data.pooling` to\ndownsample the images.\n\n---\n\n## Verification\n\n1. **Config still parses:** `load_flare_config(\"./configs/config_script.yaml\")` in cell 5.\n2. **Shapes,** from the uncommented cell 13: `ts (2, 13, 2, 4096, 4096)`,\n   `forecast (2, 13, 1, 4096, 4096)`.\n3. **Checkpoint load,** cell 19 prints `Loaded 159 / 159 pretrained weights.`\n   157 means the `head_` candidate fix did not take.\n4. **Forward pass,** cell 23: `output.shape == (2, 13, 4096, 4096)`.\n5. **Metrics run,** cells 28/30/32 return finite scalars.\n6. **Plot #1 (before training)** shows a recognizably solar prediction \u2014 that is what proves the\n   pretrained decoder was loaded. If it is noise, go back to step 3.\n7. **`trainer.fit`** completes 2 epochs on `max_samples: 10` without a shape error, and\n   `val_loss` is logged (ModelCheckpoint monitors it).\n8. **Plot #2 (after training)** \u2014 compare the difference panel against plot #1.\n9. **Regression check on the shared code:** `pytest tests/test_lora_setup.py -v` must still pass\n   (it pins the 1D trainable-parameter counts, which the `utils.py` and\n   `pl_simple_baseline.py` edits must not disturb), and `1_baseline_template.ipynb` cell 33\n   must still construct `FlareLightningModule` with a scalar target.\n"
            },
