      "time": 1789598295.499517,
      "body": "you're asking me to approve the plan but its written in code so its not easily interpretable by a human--i dont think i understand what the plan is that way. \n\nalso, my flips are literaly vertical flips over the origin (x-axis) as determined by the plots i used to check--i think the sign issue you're mentioning is something we did on purpose. \nthe decoder not being loaded, and the builders issue: idk what this means. the whole point of this experiment is just to see what happens when we use both flipped and not flipped stacks mixed together throughout the whole data (training, validation AND testing). like its to see what happens when we use both \"versions\" of data that can be observed. we're simulating hale's law, with the polarity flips. because we dont have 2 solar cycle's worth of data, we have to use 1 solar cycle and just say that the polarity could be either one way or the other. the goal is to see how the model behaves when we use both kinds of data (flipped or not here means one version of a typical solar cycle or the other). \n\nnone of the info you gave me kind of makes sense to me which is why im asking for more clarity and a better plan moving forward."
    },
    {
      "mentions": [],
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "type": "msg",
      "time": 1789598345.4398088,
      "body": "You're right \u2014 that plan was written for a compiler, not a person. Let me fix both the plan and my framing.\n\n**First, I had your experiment wrong.** I read it as \"score the same image both ways and see if the model flinches.\" You're doing something different and simpler: you only have one solar cycle of data, so you include both polarity orientations *everywhere* \u2014 train, validation, test \u2014 as a stand-in for having two cycles. Under that design, random per-sample flipping is the right mechanism, and the fact that `builders.py` gives the same flip setting to train and validation is exactly what you want, not a bug. Scratch that concern. Same for the `hmi_by` sign \u2014 you've told me it's deliberate, so it's settled.\n\n**Second, plain English on the one thing that is genuinely broken.** Your model has two parts: the big pretrained Surya body, and a small final layer that turns Surya's internal numbers back into a 13-channel picture. The checkpoint file on disk *does* contain a trained version of that final layer. But the loading code looks for it under the wrong name, doesn't find it, and skips it without complaining \u2014 that's what `157 / 159` means. So right now **the picture-drawing layer of your model is random numbers.** Everything upstream is pretrained; the last step is untrained. That's why your loss sat at 0.94 and barely moved. It's a ~5-line notebook cell to copy those two missing pieces in, and it's the difference between \"predictions look like static\" and \"predictions look like the Sun on step one.\"\n\n**Third, the one notebook change that actually answers your question.** You already record `was_flipped` per sample \u2014 but the notebook averages every sample into a single `val_loss`, which throws away the flipped/unflipped distinction. So the number you're staring at cannot tell you what you want to know. You need a per-sample table with `was_flipped` next to the error, then compare the two groups. That's the whole analysis.\n\nLet me rewrite the plan that way.",
      "id": "d427f957-8079-4b32-a14b-43112abe7d7b",
      "raw_time": false
    },
    {
      "mentions": [],
      "raw_time": false,
      "id": "7c89ff03-aec0-49ac-90d0-a5db86ec5fa0",
      "type": "msg",
      "metadata": {
        "tool_calls": [
          {
            "diffs": [
              {
                "new_text": "# Workshop plan: does Surya forecast images equally well for both polarity orientations?\n\n## What you're testing, in one paragraph\n\nSurya was pretrained on one solar cycle. Hale's law says active-region polarity reverses from one\ncycle to the next, so a model that learned cycle 24's polarity convention may be useless on cycle\n25. You don't have two cycles of data, so you simulate the second one: take the data you have and\nvertically flip a random half of it, which produces the opposite polarity arrangement. Mix both\nversions through training, validation and testing. Then ask: **does the model do equally well on\nflipped and unflipped samples, or does it only work on the orientation it was pretrained on?**\n\nYour notebook can already produce predictions and difference plots. What it can't do yet is answer\nthat question, because it averages every sample into one `val_loss` number and throws away which\nsamples were flipped. Fixing that is the main work.\n\n---\n\n## Three things to fix before running anything\n\n### 1. The final layer of your model is untrained (this is the big one)\n\nYour model = pretrained Surya body + a small final layer that converts Surya's internal\nrepresentation back into a 13-channel image.\n\nThe checkpoint file contains a trained version of that final layer. But the loader looks for it\nunder the wrong name, doesn't find it, and skips it silently \u2014 that's the `157 / 159` you saw. So\n**your image-drawing layer starts as random numbers.** That's why the loss sat at 0.94 and hardly\nbudged: the body knows the Sun, the last step is guessing.\n\n**Fix:** one new cell, inserted **between cell 19 (load weights) and cell 21 (LoRA)** \u2014 it has to\ngo there, because cell 21 renames everything. The cell loads the checkpoint again and copies the\ntwo missing tensors into `model.head_unembed`, with a shape check so it fails loudly if the names\never change.\n\n**How you'll know it worked:** run the plotting cell on an *untrained* model. Before the fix, the\nprediction panel is static. After the fix, it looks like the Sun. That's your gate \u2014 don't run any\nexperiments until you see a Sun.\n\n### 2. The notebook can't tell flipped from unflipped results\n\nYou already set `was_flipped` on every sample. Nothing reads it.\n\n**Fix:** an evaluation cell that loops over the validation set one sample at a time and builds a\ntable \u2014 one row per sample, with these columns:\n\n| column | what it is |\n|---|---|\n| `timestamp`, `GOES_class` | which flare this is |\n| `was_flipped` | the grouping column \u2014 **this is the point** |\n| `error` | how far the prediction is from the truth (mean squared error) |\n| `error_persistence` | error you'd get by just copying the last input image forward and calling it the prediction |\n| `error_flare_region` | error computed only over the brightest 0.1% of pixels in the true image |\n\nThen `table.groupby(\"was_flipped\").mean()` is your result. Two rows. That's the experiment.\n\n**Why the extra two columns matter:**\n\n- **`error_persistence` is your reality check.** You're forecasting 36 minutes ahead. The Sun\n  barely changes in 36 minutes, so \"the next image looks like the last one\" is a *very* strong\n  guess. If your model can't beat it, the model isn't forecasting \u2014 it's copying. Every error\n  number you report should be shown next to this one, or it means nothing.\n- **`error_flare_region` is how you answer \"did it predict the flare?\"** A flare covers roughly\n  0.06% of the disk. Whole-image error is dominated by quiet Sun, so a model that completely misses\n  the flare and a model that nails it will have nearly identical whole-image error. Masking to the\n  brightest pixels is the only way to see it.\n\nUse `batch_size=1` for this loop. Then each row is unambiguously flipped or not, and memory stays\nlow.\n\n### 3. Score every validation image twice\n\nInstead of relying on the random flip to give you a balanced validation set, take each validation\nimage and score it **twice**: once upright, once flipped. Same image, both orientations, so the two\ngroups are made of the same solar conditions and any difference is due to orientation alone rather\nthan to which samples happened to get flipped.\n\nThis costs one extra forward pass per sample and needs no changes to `flare_dataset.py` or the\nconfig \u2014 the flip is two lines in the evaluation loop. Keep `flip_probability` for *training*\n(where random mixing is what you want) and do validation this way.\n\n---\n\n## What your data actually contains\n\nI measured this against your index files and flare catalog, because it sets every experiment size.\n\n- **6,026 usable training samples** and **299 usable validation samples** (a sample = a flare with\n  all three needed frames present).\n- **Your `peak_time` change works.** The target frame lands a median of **5\u20136 minutes before each\n  flare peak**. So the image the model is asked to predict really is the flare. Good.\n- `ds_time_tolerance: 4d` looks alarming but is nearly harmless \u2014 the matching code already picks\n  the nearest frame per flare, so 4 days only lets in ~250 junk samples out of 6,279. Tightening it\n  to `12m` is a small cleanup, not a blocker. Your call.\n- **Bonus you may not know about:** your validation set has **97 samples from 2023** \u2014 actual\n  cycle 25 \u2014 alongside **148 from 2014\u201315** (cycle 24 maximum). Your training data is cycle 24\n  only. So on top of the simulated-flip experiment, you can run the *real* thing for free: train on\n  cycle 24, compare error on the 2014\u201315 samples against the 2023 samples. Same evaluation table,\n  just group by year instead of by `was_flipped`. If the flip experiment and the real-cycle\n  comparison agree, that's a much stronger result than either alone.\n- **You cannot shrink the images to go faster.** The dataset has a `pooling` option, but Surya's\n  position embedding and both spectral filters are hard-wired to a 4096\u00d74096 input. Shrinking the\n  image would silently discard those pretrained pieces. Don't touch it.\n\n---\n\n## Time budget \u2014 the honest version\n\nFrom your one completed run (5 training + 5 validation steps at batch size 2 in 8 minutes), each\nsample costs roughly **25\u201350 seconds** to train on. Evaluation-only is about 3\u00d7 cheaper.\n\nThat means **one training run of a few hundred steps is about 3 hours**, and you have room for\nabout three or four of them plus evaluation. Fine-tuning will not transform a 366M-parameter model\nat this scale \u2014 so treat the trained runs as a comparison between conditions, not as an attempt to\nbuild a good forecaster.\n\n**Calibrate before committing:** time 10 evaluation samples and 10 training steps, then rescale\neverything below from what you actually measure. If it's 2\u00d7 slower than this estimate, drop to two\ntraining runs.\n\n---\n\n## The schedule\n\n### Now, ~1 hour \u2014 get the plumbing right\nFix the final layer (#1). Add the evaluation table cell (#2, #3). Smoke-test on 10 validation\nsamples with `max_samples: 10`. Confirm the untrained prediction looks like the Sun.\n\n### Next, ~1 hour \u2014 your first real result, no training required\nRun the evaluation table on ~100 validation samples, upright and flipped, using the **pretrained\nmodel with no fine-tuning at all**.\n\nThis is worth doing first because your central question \u2014 \"does Surya handle both orientations?\" \u2014\nis a question about the pretrained model, and answering it costs one hour instead of three.\nYou'll immediately know whether flipping hurts, and whether Surya beats persistence at all.\n\n### Overnight, ~13 hours \u2014 three training runs\nIdentical settings except the flip rate, all evaluated on the same validation set:\n\n| run | `flip_probability` during training | what it tells you |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A** | `0.0` \u2014 one orientation only | baseline: the pretraining convention only |\n| **B** | `0.5` \u2014 mixed, your main experiment | can it learn both orientations at once? |\n| **C** | `1.0` \u2014 flipped only | can it learn the *other* orientation at all? |\n\nThen a fourth run if you have time: **run B again on a different set of training samples of the\nsame size**. Comparing those two gives you the \"different training sets, same validation\" /\nensemble experiment your advisor suggested, and tells you how much of any difference is just noise\nfrom which samples you happened to pick.\n\n**Practical protections for an unattended overnight job:**\n- Put the runs in a plain shell script launched with `nohup`, so a crash in run A doesn't cost you\n  runs B and C.\n- Give each `Trainer` a wall-clock cap (`max_time={\"hours\": 3}`) so no single run eats the night.\n- Save a checkpoint every epoch.\n- Keep `batch_size` at 1\u20132 and `num_workers` at 0\u20132. The crash you had earlier was **host RAM**,\n  not GPU \u2014 4 loader workers each holding two 5 GB batches in pinned memory. Two workers is the\n  ceiling here; the loader doesn't expose the prefetch depth, so workers are your only knob. Watch\n  it with `free -g` during the first run.\n\n### Morning, ~4 hours \u2014 analysis\nCombine the four tables. Two headline comparisons:\n1. `error` grouped by `was_flipped`, per run, always shown next to `error_persistence`.\n2. `error` grouped by year (2014\u201315 vs 2023), which is the real cycle comparison.\n\nPlus the input/truth/prediction/difference figure you already built, for two or three hand-picked\nM-class flares \u2014 one that worked and one that didn't.\n\n### One config fix before the overnight runs\n`configs/config_script.yaml` line 148 lists `target_modules: [q_proj, v_proj, k_proj, out_proj,\nfc1, fc2]`. Four of those six names don't exist anywhere in Surya \u2014 they're from a different model\nlibrary. The LoRA code only complains if *nothing* matches, and `fc1`/`fc2` do match, so it runs\nhappily while **never adapting any attention layer**. The correct list for Surya is\n`[fc1, fc2, attn.qkv, attn.proj]`. Change it and the printed trainable-parameter count will go up \u2014\nthat's your confirmation.\n\n---\n\n## How to read the results\n\n- **Flipped and unflipped errors come out about equal, and both beat persistence** \u2192 the model\n  handles both polarity orientations fine. Hale's law is not the explanation for the cycle-25\n  problem, and you've ruled out a leading hypothesis. That's a real result.\n- **Flipped error is clearly worse than unflipped** \u2192 the model is tied to the orientation it was\n  pretrained on. This directly supports the Hale's-law explanation. Then run B vs. run A tells you\n  whether mixing both orientations during training fixes it.\n- **Run C (flipped-only training) does as well as run A** \u2192 the model can learn either orientation\n  fine, it just can't hold both at once. Different problem, still interesting.\n- **Nothing beats persistence anywhere** \u2192 say so first and plainly. At a 36-minute horizon\n  persistence is genuinely hard to beat. Also worth knowing: mean-squared-error training pushes a\n  model toward the *average* likely image, which systematically blurs out small bright features \u2014\n  exactly what a flare is. So a blurry, flare-free prediction is partly a property of the loss\n  function, not a bug in your setup. That's why `error_flare_region` is in the table.\n\n---\n\n## Verification checklist\n\n1. Untrained prediction looks like the Sun, not static \u2192 the final-layer fix took.\n2. Flipping a sample twice returns the original (`flip(flip(x)) == x`) \u2192 your flip is a clean\n   involution and the eval loop isn't corrupting anything.\n3. `error_persistence` is identical for the upright and flipped scoring of the same sample \u2014 it\n   doesn't involve the model, so if it differs, the flip is being applied inconsistently.\n4. The validation set has 299 samples; the year subsets have 148 (2014\u201315) and 97 (2023). Different\n   numbers mean the tolerance or the input offsets changed.\n5. All runs report the same validation-set size in their logs, or they aren't comparable.\n6. `free -g` stays well under 60 GB through the first run of each type.",
                "path": "/home/jovyan/.claude/plans/i-need-help-changing-elegant-codd.md",
                "old_text": "# Validation & experiment plan: does Surya forecast images across the Hale reversal?\n\n## Context\n\nThe 2D conversion of `downstream_apps/flare_forecast/2_finetune_2D-image-flip.ipynb` is **done and\nverified** (5.3 M trainable / 371 M total, `max_epochs=2 reached`, `train_loss 0.940` /\n`val_loss 0.938`, prediction-vs-truth-vs-difference plots working). This plan covers what to do\nwith the remaining ~20 usable hours of the workshop.\n\nThe scientific question is: **Surya was pretrained on cycle 24; it appears not to forecast\nusefully in cycle 25, and the suspected cause is Hale's polarity law.** A north\u2013south mirror of\nthe image is the cheap proxy for the cycle flip \u2014 mirroring a cycle-24 northern active region\ninto the south preserves the east\u2013west ordering of its bipole but moves it to a hemisphere where\ncycle 24 had the *opposite* leading polarity, i.e. it produces a configuration that looks like a\nvalid **cycle-25** region. That is why \"flipped vs unflipped\" is the right experiment.\n\nSecondary questions: shorter input spacing (`[-60, -36]` instead of Surya's ~2 h/1 h), and\n`peak_time` anchoring so the target frame is the flare itself.\n\n---\n\n## What the data actually allows (measured, not assumed)\n\nRun against `data/indices/surya_aws_s3_{train,val}.csv` and `data/hek_flare_catalog.csv`:\n\n| | matched samples | M/X | notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| train index | **6,026** | 537 | years 2010\u20132021; **no 2022, no 2023** |\n| val index | **299** | 28 | years 2011\u20132023, **including 97 samples in 2023** |\n\n- **The val set already contains the experiment.** 2014+2015 \u2192 **148 cycle-24-maximum samples**;\n  **2023 \u2192 97 cycle-25 samples (9 M/X)**. Train is cycle 24 only. So train-on-24 / test-on-25 is\n  available with **zero new index building**.\n- The flare catalog **ends 2023-02-20** and the Surya index ends 2024-12-31 and skips 2022. So\n  \"current cycle\" means **2023** here, not 2025/26. Say so in the write-up.\n- **`peak_time` anchoring works as intended.** `flare_dataset.py:84-106` does\n  `merge_asof(direction=\"forward\")` then keeps the smallest `|delta|` per flare, so the reference\n  timestep is the Surya frame immediately *before* each peak \u2014 **median offset 5\u20136 minutes**. The\n  target frame really is the flare. `ds_time_tolerance: 4d` is *not* the disaster it looks like\n  (it only admits ~250 extra samples out of 6,279, because the dedupe already picks the nearest);\n  tightening to `12m` is a nice-to-have, not a blocker.\n- **Downsampling is impossible.** `HelioNetCDFDataset` has a `pooling` argument\n  (`helio.py:409, 908`), but the checkpoint's `embedding.pos_embed` is `(1, 65536, 1280)` and both\n  `blocks_spectral_gating.*.filter.complex_weight` are `(256, 129, 1280, 2)` \u2014 hard-wired to the\n  256\u00d7256 token grid. Shrinking the image silently drops the position embedding *and* both\n  spectral filters at load time. **Do not use `pooling`.**\n- **The warm cache does not help.** `/home/jovyan/scratch_space/surya_cache` holds 852 timestamped\n  files (467 GB, EFS, effectively unlimited space) but only **42** of them form a complete\n  `(-60, -36, 0)` triple. Nothing to exploit; but cache *does* persist across epochs, so epoch 2+\n  of any run is IO-free.\n- **Cost model.** The verified run did 5 train + 5 val steps at batch 2 in 7 m 58 s\n  \u21d2 **~25\u201350 s per sample-pass**, gradient-checkpointed on an L40S. Budget **~1,500 train-step\n  sample-passes for the entire remaining workshop.** Forward-only eval (`no_grad`, no optimizer\n  state) is ~3\u00d7 cheaper, ~10\u201315 s/sample.\n\n### The consequence that reframes everything\n\nFine-tuning at this budget will barely move a 366 M-parameter model. But **the question\n\"does Surya forecast images, flipped or not?\" is a question about the *pretrained* model \u2014 it\nneeds no training at all.** Zero-shot evaluation is 3\u00d7 cheaper per sample and answers the actual\nhypothesis. So: **spend the day on a zero-shot evaluation grid, spend the night on the four\ntraining runs your advisor asked for.**\n\n---\n\n## Blocker to fix first: the decoder is not being loaded\n\n`load_pretrained_weights` (`workshop_infrastructure/utils.py:404`) tries only the keys `k` and\n`backbone.{k}`. The 2D head's key is `head_unembed.unembed.0.weight`, so the checkpoint's\n`unembed.unembed.0.{weight,bias}` \u2014 shapes `(3328, 1280, 1, 1)` and `(3328,)`, where\n3328 = 16\u00b2\u00d713, i.e. **exactly our `LinearDecoder`** \u2014 is silently dropped. Cell 19 reports\n`157 / 159` and **the head stays at random init.**\n\nZero-shot evaluation of a random readout is meaningless, so this must be fixed before Tier 1.\n\n- **Do it notebook-locally** (respects \"notebook only\"): a new cell **between cells 19 and 21**,\n  before PEFT renames anything, that loads those two tensors into `model.head_unembed` by name\n  with a shape assertion. Re-check: the count should become **159 / 159** equivalent.\n- The clean fix is one line in `utils.py` \u2014 `for candidate in (k, f\"backbone.{k}\", f\"head_{k}\"):`,\n  guarded by the existing shape check so it cannot affect the 1D path. Worth a PR upstream, but\n  keep it out of this workshop's critical path unless you want to run\n  `pytest tests/test_lora_setup.py -v` to confirm nothing shifted.\n\n**Sanity gate:** with the decoder loaded, the *untrained* prediction panel must show a\nrecognizable Sun. If it is noise, stop \u2014 the load did not take.\n\n---\n\n## Design decision: flip in the evaluation loop, not in the dataset\n\n`flare_dataset.py:147-153` draws `np.random.rand() < flip_probability` per `__getitem__`. That is\nwrong for this experiment, three ways:\n\n- **It is random, so it is not paired.** You need the *same* validation sample scored both ways.\n- **`builders.py:104`** builds `common = {**_base_dataset_kwargs(...), **task_kwargs}` and hands\n  the *same* `task_kwargs` to both train and val, so one `build_helio_dataloaders` call can never\n  give you flipped-val and unflipped-train.\n- **`hmi_by` has the wrong sign.** Under y \u2192 \u2212y a vector field transforms as\n  `bx \u2192 bx`, `by \u2192 \u2212by`, `bz \u2192 bz`. The code flips all 13 channel arrays with no sign correction,\n  so the mirrored stack is not a physical mirror \u2014 and polarity is the entire point of the\n  experiment. `hmi_m`, `hmi_bz`, `hmi_v` are line-of-sight and correctly unchanged; only\n  **`hmi_by` (channel index 10)** needs negating.\n\n**Instead: keep `flip_probability=0.0` and apply the transforms explicitly in the eval loop.** One\ndownloaded batch, several forward passes, perfectly paired, zero dataset or infra changes \u2014 and it\nunlocks the metric you actually want (below). Define in the notebook:\n\n```\nT_id(x)       = x\nT_mirror(x)   = flip(x, dim=-2)                       # geometry only (what the dataset does today)\nT_hale(x)     = flip(x, dim=-2), then negate hmi_by    # physically correct mirror\nT_polinv(x)   = negate hmi_m, hmi_bx, hmi_by, hmi_bz   # pure cycle-24 \u2192 cycle-25 polarity reversal\n```\n\n**Sign flips happen in normalized space**, and the pipeline is signum-log *then* z-score, so the\nexact operation is `z \u2192 \u2212z \u2212 2\u03bc/\u03c3`, not `z \u2192 \u2212z`. Checked the scalers: for `hmi_m/bx/by/bz`,\n`2\u03bc/\u03c3 \u2264 0.006`, so plain negation is accurate to <0.6 % of a standard deviation \u2014 fine, but\ninclude the `\u22122\u03bc/\u03c3` term since the constants are already in `cfg`/`scalers`. Never negate an AIA\nchannel (`2\u03bc/\u03c3 = 2.41` for `aia171`, and intensities are not signed).\n\nEvery `T` here is an involution, so `T\u207b\u00b9 = T`.\n\nAlso note `HelioNetCDFDataset` has its **own** `random_vert_flip` (`helio.py:410, 650-653`) at a\nhardcoded p=0.5. `_base_dataset_kwargs` never forwards it so it is inert today \u2014 just don't turn\nit on, or you will get double-flipped samples.\n\n---\n\n## Metrics: what to record per sample\n\nWhole-disk MSE is dominated by quiet Sun, which is why the current `train_loss \u2248 val_loss \u2248 0.94`\ntells you almost nothing. Reuse `FlareMetrics` (`metrics/flare_metrics.py` \u2014 already flattens with\n`reshape(-1)`, so it works on images unchanged) but record a **row per sample** with:\n\n| column | why |\n|---|---|\n| `ds_index`, `year`, `GOES_class`, `CMX`, `cycle` (24 / 25) | the grouping axes |\n| `mse_id`, `mse_mirror`, `mse_hale`, `mse_polinv` | prediction vs. the *unflipped* ground truth, after mapping the prediction back with `T\u207b\u00b9` |\n| **`mse_persistence`** | copy the \"now\" frame `ts[:, :, -1]` forward. **This is the skill reference.** A forecast that cannot beat persistence at 36 minutes has no skill, no matter how good the picture looks. |\n| **`equivariance`** = `MSE(T(model(T(x))), model(x))` | **the central metric for your hypothesis.** \u22480 \u21d2 the model is mirror-equivariant, so flipping cannot help or hurt and Hale's law cannot be the mechanism. Large \u21d2 the model's answer genuinely depends on hemisphere/polarity, and the hypothesis is live. You do not have this number yet. |\n| `mse_flare_region` | MSE restricted to the top-0.1 % brightest pixels of the ground-truth `aia94`/`aia1600` frame. **This is how you answer \"did it predict the flare?\"** \u2014 a flare is ~0.06 % of the disk, so it is invisible in the whole-disk number. |\n\nThen the headline table is `groupby([\"cycle\", \"transform\"])` on skill score\n`1 \u2212 mse_model/mse_persistence`. Keep the existing input/truth/prediction/difference plot for 2\u20133\nhand-picked samples as the qualitative figure; the table is the result.\n\nPut these helpers in a **new file `downstream_apps/flare_forecast/evaluation.py`** (your own app\ndirectory, not shared infra) so the notebook and the overnight script use identical code.\n\n---\n\n## Tier 1 \u2014 zero-shot evaluation grid (~2.5 h, do this awake)\n\nNo training. `model.eval()`, `torch.no_grad()`, `batch_size=1`.\n\n- Val subsets, built by filtering the existing val dataset's `valid_indices` by year:\n  **cycle 24 = 2014+2015 (148 samples)**, **cycle 25 = 2023 (97 samples)**.\n- Transforms: `T_id`, `T_hale`, `T_polinv` (skip `T_mirror` \u2014 `T_hale` is the same geometry done\n  correctly; add `T_mirror` only if you want to quantify the `hmi_by` bug).\n- 245 samples \u00d7 3 forwards \u2248 735 passes \u2248 2.5 h.\n\n**This is the deliverable.** It answers, for the pretrained model: does it forecast at all (vs.\npersistence), is it worse in cycle 25 than cycle 24, and does mirroring or polarity-inverting the\ninput recover the cycle-25 performance.\n\n## Tier 2 \u2014 input-interval comparison (~1 h, optional)\n\n`[-60, -36]` (current) vs `[-60, -24]` vs `[-120, -60]` (Surya's pretraining spacing). Each needs\nits own dataset build because `valid_indices` depends on which offsets exist \u2014 so **intersect the\nthree `valid_indices` sets and evaluate only on the intersection**, or the comparison is confounded\nby different samples. Trim to ~60 samples per cycle \u00d7 2 transforms \u2248 240 passes \u2248 1 h.\n\nNote `[-120, -60]` is the fairest test of \"is Surya broken in cycle 25\", because it is the spacing\nthe checkpoint was trained on. If cycle-25 performance is bad even there, the interval change is\nnot the cause.\n\n## Tier 3 \u2014 four fine-tuning runs (~13 h, run overnight)\n\nIdentical val set (the 245 Tier-1 samples) for all four, so they are directly comparable. Batch 1,\nmatched **step count** (not epoch count) so data size is not confounded with training length.\n\n| run | train set | flip aug | answers |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **A** | 64 cycle-24 samples \u00d7 4 epochs = 256 steps | none | baseline vs. fine-tune on identical sets |\n| **B** | same 64 samples \u00d7 4 epochs | 50 % `T_hale` | does flip augmentation buy cycle-25 skill? |\n| **C** | 256 different cycle-24 samples \u00d7 1 epoch = 256 steps | none | **different amount of training data, same validation** |\n| **D** | 64 samples from *different years* \u00d7 4 epochs | none | **different training set, same validation \u2192 ensemble member with A** |\n\n\u22482.5 h each + ~50 min val each \u2248 13 h, inside the 16-hour sleep window with margin.\n\n- Run **B**'s train-time flip is the one place `flip_probability` is the right tool \u2014 but fix the\n  `hmi_by` sign first, and because `builders.py:104` shares `task_kwargs`, build the val loader\n  from a **second** `build_helio_dataloaders` call with `flip_probability=0.0`.\n- Wrap all four in one `nohup`'d shell script calling\n  `downstream_apps/flare_forecast/4_run_experiments.py` (new file, your app dir) so a crash in one\n  run does not cost you the night. Set `Trainer(max_time={\"hours\": 3})` and `ModelCheckpoint`\n  every epoch for the same reason.\n- **Fix the LoRA config before Tier 3.** `configs/config_script.yaml:148` has\n  `target_modules: [q_proj, v_proj, k_proj, out_proj, fc1, fc2]` \u2014 those are HuggingFace names.\n  Only `fc1`/`fc2` match anything in Surya, and PEFT raises only when *nothing* matches, so\n  **attention is silently never adapted**. Per `CLAUDE.md` it should be\n  `[fc1, fc2, attn.qkv, attn.proj]`. Verify against the `[LoRA] Adapted modules` list printed at\n  startup; the trainable count will rise.\n\n## Memory recipe (stay well under 60 GB)\n\nThe earlier kernel death was **host RAM**, not GPU: 4 workers \u00d7 prefetch 2 \u00d7 5.2 GB/batch of\n`pin_memory=True` (non-swappable). `build_helio_dataloaders` does not expose `prefetch_factor`\n(defaults to 2) and sets `persistent_workers=True`, so workers are the knob.\n\n- `batch_size: 1` for eval, `1` for training. One `ts` is 1.7 GB, one 13\u00d74096\u00d74096 fp32\n  prediction is 0.87 GB.\n- `num_workers=0` for eval (proven safe), at most `2` for training.\n- Accumulate **scalars** into the results DataFrame; only materialize full images for the 2\u20133\n  samples you actually plot, and `del` them after.\n- Keep `bf16-mixed` and `checkpoint_layers: [0..9]`.\n- Watch it with `nvidia-smi` and `free -g` in a side terminal during the first run of each tier.\n\n---\n\n## Verification\n\n1. **Decoder loaded** \u2014 the new cell between 19 and 21 reports both `unembed` tensors copied, and\n   the untrained prediction panel shows a recognizable Sun, not noise. Hard gate for Tier 1.\n2. **Transforms are involutions** \u2014 assert `T(T(x)) \u2248 x` for each `T` on one real batch\n   (`atol` a few times `2\u03bc/\u03c3`), and assert `T_hale` changes `hmi_by` but leaves `aia171` untouched\n   apart from the flip.\n3. **Sample counts** \u2014 the year-filtered val subsets have exactly **148** (2014+2015) and **97**\n   (2023) samples. Different numbers mean the tolerance or offsets changed.\n4. **Timing calibration first** \u2014 before committing to the schedule, time 10 eval samples and 10\n   train steps and rescale the whole plan from the measured rate. The 25\u201350 s/pass figure is\n   inferred from one progress bar; if it is 2\u00d7 worse, drop Tier 2 and cut Tier 3 to runs A and C.\n5. **Persistence sanity** \u2014 `mse_persistence` must be finite and the *same* for every transform\n   (it does not depend on the model). If it varies, the `T\u207b\u00b9` mapping is wrong.\n6. **Sample sets match across Tier 2** \u2014 `len(intersection)` printed, and every interval variant\n   evaluated on exactly that set.\n7. **Tier 3 comparability** \u2014 all four runs log the same `val_loss` key on the same 245 samples;\n   confirm identical `len(val_loader)` in each run's log.\n8. If you touch `utils.py` or the YAML: `pytest tests/test_lora_setup.py -v` still passes.\n\n## Expected outcomes, so you know what you are looking at\n\n- **`equivariance` \u2248 0** \u21d2 the model treats the mirrored Sun identically; Hale's law cannot explain\n  a cycle-25 failure, and the flip experiment is a null result (still publishable as a negative).\n- **`equivariance` large AND `mse_hale` < `mse_id` on cycle-25 samples** \u21d2 the headline positive\n  result: presenting cycle-25 regions in cycle-24 polarity convention recovers forecast skill.\n- **Nothing beats persistence anywhere** \u21d2 report that first and honestly. At a 36-minute horizon\n  persistence is very strong, and MSE's optimum is the conditional mean, so an MSE-trained image\n  model systematically blurs and under-predicts exactly the compact bright features a flare is.\n  That is a property of the loss, not a bug in your setup \u2014 and it is the reason\n  `mse_flare_region` is on the table above."
              }
