    {
      "id": "b083b7e9-156a-4323-9b94-a1a6c0bb7924",
      "mentions": [],
      "raw_time": false,
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "time": 1789649381.473552,
      "type": "msg",
      "body": "Both answers, then a test cell you can paste.\n\n## `num_workers`\n\nIt's the number of **background processes that fetch and prepare data while the GPU computes.** Nothing more \u2014 it has zero effect on results, only on speed and memory.\n\n- `num_workers=0` \u2192 no background processes. The main process downloads the NetCDF files from S3, normalizes them, then hands them to the GPU. GPU sits idle during loading. Memory use is minimal and predictable.\n- `num_workers=4` \u2192 four processes each independently loading batches ahead of time, so the GPU rarely waits.\n\n**Why it killed your kernel.** Here's the actual arithmetic for your config:\n\n| | size |\n|---|---|\n| one `ts` at batch 2 (2 \u00d7 13ch \u00d7 2 frames \u00d7 4096 \u00d7 4096 \u00d7 4 bytes) | **3.5 GB** |\n| one `forecast` at batch 2 | **1.7 GB** |\n| \u2192 one batch | **~5.2 GB** |\n\nEach worker queues up to **2 batches ahead** (a PyTorch default that `build_helio_dataloaders` doesn't expose), so each worker holds ~10.5 GB. Four workers \u2248 **42 GB** \u2014 and it's pinned memory, which cannot be swapped to disk. Add the validation loader keeping its own workers alive permanently, plus the model, and you were over the line. That's the crash.\n\nRule of thumb for your setup: **0 for anything interactive, at most 2 for the overnight runs**, and watch `free -g`.\n\n## `max_number_of_samples`\n\nIt caps how many samples the dataset has. Two details that matter more than they look:\n\n1. **It takes the *first* N, not a random N.** In `flare_dataset.py:119-122` it's a plain `[:N]` slice of a list that's sorted by flare peak time. So `max_number_of_samples=3` \u2014 what you have right now in cell 11 \u2014 gives you the **three earliest matched flares in your index, all from 2010.** Not a representative sample of anything. Fine for shape-checking, misleading for anything else.\n2. **It's applied after flare matching**, so it counts *usable* samples (flares with all three frames present), not raw index rows. Your full pools are 6,026 train and 299 validation.\n\nWhen we get to real runs we'll want a random or year-stratified subset instead of the first N. Worth knowing now so a \"150-sample run\" doesn't quietly mean \"150 consecutive flares from one week in 2010.\"\n\n## Forcing a flipped forward pass and timing it\n\n**Quick way:** add `flip_probability=1.0` to the cell 11 arguments \u2014 every sample gets flipped. Good for confirming the plumbing works end to end, but it mixes S3 download time into your measurement, so it's bad for timing.\n\n**Better way for timing:** reuse one already-loaded batch and flip the tensor directly, so you're measuring only the model. Paste this as a **new cell right after cell 13** (it needs `batch` from cell 13 and `model` from cell 17, so run 17 first):\n\n```python\n# Timing test: does a vertically flipped input cost more than an upright one?\n# Uses one already-loaded batch, so no S3 time leaks into the measurement.\nimport time, torch\n\ndev = \"cuda\" if torch.cuda.is_available() else \"cpu\"\nmodel_t = model.to(dev).eval()\n\n# The backbone only reads these three keys \u2014 moving just them keeps memory low.\nb1 = {k: batch[k][:1].to(dev) for k in (\"ts\", \"time_delta_input\", \"lead_time_delta\")}\n\nb1_flip = dict(b1)\nt0 = time.perf_counter()\nb1_flip[\"ts\"] = torch.flip(b1[\"ts\"], dims=[-2])   # dim -2 is H: the same vertical flip the dataset does\nflip_cost = time.perf_counter() - t0\n\ndef timed_forward(b, label, reps=2):\n    with torch.no_grad(), torch.autocast(device_type=dev, dtype=torch.bfloat16):\n        model_t(b)                              # warmup: first call includes one-off setup\n        if dev == \"cuda\": torch.cuda.synchronize()\n        times = []\n        for _ in range(reps):\n            t = time.perf_counter()\n            out = model_t(b)\n            if dev == \"cuda\": torch.cuda.synchronize()\n            times.append(time.perf_counter() - t)\n    print(f\"{label:10s} forward: {min(times):6.2f} s   (out {tuple(out.shape)})\")\n    return min(times)\n\nif dev == \"cuda\": torch.cuda.reset_peak_memory_stats()\nprint(f\"flip itself: {flip_cost*1000:.0f} ms\")\nt_up   = timed_forward(b1,      \"upright\")\nt_flip = timed_forward(b1_flip, \"flipped\")\nprint(f\"\\nratio flipped/upright: {t_flip/t_up:.3f}\")\nif dev == \"cuda\":\n    print(f\"peak GPU memory: {torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated()/2**30:.1f} GB\")\n```\n\n**What I expect it to print, and why:** the ratio should be **1.00 within noise**, and the flip itself should be tens of milliseconds. The model performs exactly the same arithmetic no matter what the pixel values are \u2014 there's no data-dependent branching, no early exit, no shortcut for dark pixels. Flipping only rearranges where numbers sit in memory.\n\nSo if the ratio comes back near 1.0, that's the correct answer, not a broken test. It's still worth running, because it tells you the *absolute* per-sample forward cost on your GPU \u2014 which is the number my whole \"1 hour \u2248 300 evaluated samples\" estimate rests on, and I'd rather you measure it than trust my arithmetic off one progress bar.\n\nOne caveat: run this **before** the LoRA cell (21) or after, but note it down \u2014 LoRA wraps the model and adds a little overhead, so times taken before and after aren't directly comparable.\n\nWant me to run the equivalent as a standalone script so you get the numbers without spending notebook time on it?"
    },
