      "id": "f8c4a378-3a73-4744-9c01-57422282dbf7",
      "body": "Understood \u2014 no new files. Here's everything as notebook cells and one-line config edits instead.\n\n**Three edits you can make right now:**\n\n1. `configs/config_script.yaml:127` \u2014 `checkpoint_layers: [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]` instead of all ten. Halves the recompute overhead. Watch peak GPU memory; if it fits, try `[0, 1]`.\n2. `configs/config_script.yaml:98` \u2014 `s3_boto3_max_concurrency: 16` instead of `4`. Four times the threads per download, which is what matters while the cache is cold.\n3. In cell 3, next to the other torch setup: `torch.set_float32_matmul_precision(\"medium\")`. The training script does this and your notebook doesn't.\n\n**Pre-warming as a notebook cell.** Paste after cell 11 (needs `train_data_loader`). It pulls samples through the dataset's own loading path, so the files land exactly where the dataset will look for them, then throws the arrays away:\n\n```python\n# Warm the S3 cache before training, so the GPU isn't waiting on the network mid-run.\n# Downloads parallelize; training doesn't. Doing them separately is strictly faster.\nimport time\nfrom concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor\n\nds = train_data_loader.dataset\n\ndef fetch(i):\n    _ = ds[i]        # triggers download + normalize; discard immediately\n    return i\n\nt0 = time.perf_counter()\n# 4 threads max: each in-flight sample holds ~2.6 GB, so 4 is ~10 GB \u2014 well under your ceiling.\nwith ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=4) as pool:\n    for n, _ in enumerate(pool.map(fetch, range(len(ds))), 1):\n        el = time.perf_counter() - t0\n        print(f\"\\r{n}/{len(ds)}  {el/60:.1f} min elapsed, \"\n              f\"~{(len(ds)-n)*el/n/60:.1f} min left\", end=\"\", flush=True)\nprint(f\"\\ncache warm in {(time.perf_counter()-t0)/60:.1f} min\")\n```\n\nRun it once for the training set and once for `val_data_loader.dataset`. Then **re-run the same cell** \u2014 the second pass should finish in seconds, because everything is local. That's your confirmation the cache is being hit.\n\nOnce it's warm, `num_workers=2` in cell 11 becomes safe (loading is a local disk read, not a 1.6 GB download), and *that's* when the GPU trace should fill in.\n\n**One thing this will tell you that we don't currently know.** Run your timing test from the last message on a warm cache and compare the step time to what you saw cold. If a warm step is much faster, S3 was the bottleneck and my \"~3 hours per training run\" estimate was too pessimistic \u2014 you can afford more data. If it's about the same, the GPU was already the limit, the flat trace is mostly the *validation* phase and startup, and the checkpointing change in edit #1 becomes your main lever.\n\nWorth measuring before you commit the overnight schedule, since it changes how much data you can use by possibly a large factor.",
