            "raw_output": "total cells: 50\n  0 mark | # PyTorch Lightning fine-tuning template\n  1 mark | ## Set your cuda visible device\n  2 code | import os\n  3 code | # Must be set BEFORE torch is imported: cuBLAS reads this once, when it initializes, so\n  4 mark | ## Load configuration\n  5 code | # The config is the single source of truth. load_flare_config() parses it into a typed\n  6 mark | ## Download assets\n  7 code | # One implementation, shared by the notebooks, the training script and the\n  8 mark | ### Typed configuration, and how to extend it for your own task\n  9 mark | ## Define Downstream (DS) datasets\n 10 code | from downstream_apps.flare_forecast.datasets.flare_dataset import flare_Dataset\n 11 code | # build_helio_dataloaders() constructs the train and validation datasets and wraps them\n 12 mark | Training and validation get separate datasets and dataloaders. They differ only in the i\n 13 code | # Inspect a single batch to confirm shapes before building the model.\n 14 mark | ## Initialize the HelioSpectformer model\n 15 code | from workshop_infrastructure.models.finetune_models import HelioSpectformer2D\n 16 mark | Now the config file really comes into bear. The Spectformer has a metric ton of hyperpar\n 17 code | # HelioSpectformer2D has a long list of architecture arguments, and all of them come\n 18 mark | ## Load model weights\n 19 code | # The checkpoint was saved from HelioSpectFormer directly, so its keys are flat\n 20 mark | ## To LoRA or not to Lora\n 21 code | # Three fine-tuning regimes, all selected from the model: section of the config:\n 22 mark | We can now test that this model manipulates a batch as expected and returns an estimate \n 23 code | batch = next(iter(train_data_loader))\n 24 mark | ## Define your metrics\n 25 code | from downstream_apps.flare_forecast.metrics.flare_metrics import FlareMetrics\n 26 code | train_loss_metrics = FlareMetrics(\"train_loss\")\n 27 mark | Now they can be evaluated in our model's output and our ground truth.   First the loss t\n 28 code | # batch[\"forecast\"] is (B, 13, 1, 4096, 4096) \u2014 the 1 is the lead-time axis.\n 29 mark | Then a training evaluation that will not backpropagate and inform our model, but that we\n 30 code | train_evaluation_metrics(output, target)\n 31 mark | In the validation evaluation metrics we report both MSE and RRSE\n 32 code | validation_evaluation_metrics(output, target)\n 33 mark | ## Define your PyTorch ligthning module\n 34 code | from downstream_apps.flare_forecast.lightning_modules.pl_flare_baseline import FlareLigh\n 35 code | class Flare2DLightningModule(FlareLightningModule):\n 36 mark | ## Set your global seeds\n 37 code | L.seed_everything(42, workers=True)\n 38 mark | ## Intialize Lightning module\n 39 code | metrics = {\n 40 mark | ## Logging\n 41 code | project_name = cfg.wandb_project\n 42 mark | ## Initialize trainer\n 43 code | max_epochs = 2\n 44 mark | ## Fit the model\n 45 code | trainer.fit(lit_model, train_data_loader, val_data_loader)\n 46 code | (empty)\n 47 code | # ============================================================================\n 48 code | import numpy as np\n 49 code | (empty)\nShell cwd was reset to /home/jovyan/tmp/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/flare_forecast/old-chats-notebooks",
