    },
    {
      "time": 1789650567.0189004,
      "body": "Set to `0.5`. Three things that follow from that, plainly:\n\n**It applies to validation too.** `build_helio_datasets` hands the same task kwargs to both splits, so val gets flipped at the same rate. For your design \u2014 both orientations mixed through everything \u2014 that's what you want, so I left it.\n\n**The flip is re-rolled on every read, not fixed per sample.** A given flare is upright in epoch 1 and possibly flipped in epoch 2. That's fine for training (it's ordinary augmentation) but it means `val_loss` wobbles between epochs for reasons that have nothing to do with learning, and two runs aren't strictly comparable. When you get to the real runs, the fix is to make validation deterministic \u2014 seed the flip off the sample index instead of `np.random.rand()`, or score every val image both ways. That's the thing standing between you and a trustworthy flipped-vs-unflipped number, and it's the next edit I'd make.\n\n**With `max_number_of_samples=3` you may get 0 or 3 flipped by chance.** Coin flips on three samples. Fine for the plumbing check; don't read anything into the loss.",
      "raw_time": false,
      "mentions": [],
      "id": "5295d831-ece0-468f-86d1-ba5e2c37bcee",
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "type": "msg"
    },
    {
