    },
    {
      "raw_time": false,
      "sender": "jpereira",
      "body": "can we set the flip inside the notebook or do i need to change flare_dataset.py itself? the real check i need is a small small example of just seeing what happens when its flipped in the pipeline (training/val/test) BEFORE the longer run ... i need to confirm this test worked and whatnot",
      "metadata": {
        "model": {
          "settings": {},
          "id": null
        },
        "settings": {},
        "to_persona": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona"
      },
      "id": "686bad01-1d97-4c52-8519-193b77846b60",
      "time": 1789650660.8786206,
      "type": "msg"
    },
    {
      "body": "Notebook only \u2014 no `flare_dataset.py` change needed. The dataset already takes `flip_probability` (line 48) and already implements the flip (149\u2013153); the notebook just never passed it. That's the line I added to cell 11.\n\nAnd because cell 11 uses `num_workers=0`, you can also change it *mid-notebook* by setting the attribute on the dataset object \u2014 which is what makes a small check possible without rebuilding loaders. (That trick stops working once `num_workers > 0`: workers are spawned as separate processes with their own copy, so then you must set it in cell 11.)\n\nHere's the small end-to-end check:",
      "id": "76034b3f-6107-41bb-96c5-d788aca008ce",
      "mentions": [],
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "time": 1789650766.605474,
      "type": "msg",
      "raw_time": false
    },
    {
      "raw_time": false,
      "time": 1789650766.6109817,
      "metadata": {
        "tool_calls": [
          {
            "raw_input": {
              "command": "cd /home/jovyan/tmp/surya_workshop && /home/jovyan/envs/surya_ws/bin/python - <<'PYEOF'\nimport json, uuid\n\nNB = \"downstream_apps/flare_forecast/2_finetune_2D-image-flip.ipynb\"\nnb = json.load(open(NB))\n\ndef cell(kind, text):\n    c = {\"cell_type\": kind, \"metadata\": {}, \"id\": uuid.uuid4().hex[:8],\n         \"source\": text.strip(\"\\n\").splitlines(keepends=True)}\n    if kind == \"code\":\n        c[\"execution_count\"] = None\n        c[\"outputs\"] = []\n    return c\n\nMD = \"\"\"\n### The small check: flips through the real pipeline, before any long run\n\nThree questions, in order, on a handful of samples.\n\n**A. Does the dataset's flip fire, and does it flip the *target* too?** This is the one that\nmatters most. If the input were flipped but the ground-truth image were not, you would be asking\nthe model to predict a mirror image of what it was shown \u2014 guaranteed nonsense, and the loss would\nlook bad for a reason that has nothing to do with Hale's law. Asserted directly.\n\n**B. Does orientation change the answer?** Each sample is scored **twice** \u2014 once upright, once\nflipped \u2014 with both its input and its target flipped together. Same flare, both orientations, so\nany difference is orientation and not \"which samples happened to get flipped.\" That pairing is what\nmakes a 6-sample check readable; with random flipping you would need hundreds of samples to see the\nsame thing. Random mixing is still the right thing for the real training runs \u2014 this is only how\nyou measure precisely when N is tiny.\n\nEach row also carries the **persistence** error: what you'd score by just copying the last input\nframe forward. It doesn't involve the model, and flipping cannot change it, so upright and flipped\npersistence must come out **identical**. If they don't, the flip is being applied inconsistently\nsomewhere and nothing else in the table can be trusted.\n\n**C. Do the flips actually reach the training loop?** Turns the random flipping back on, pulls real\nbatches through `train_data_loader`, prints `was_flipped` per batch, and takes a real optimizer\nstep. This is the end-to-end confirmation.\n\nCost is roughly two forward passes per sample plus one read each, so it scales with\n`max_number_of_samples` in cell 11. Six samples is plenty.\n\"\"\"\n\nCODE = '''\n# Small end-to-end flip check. Runs at batch size 1 throughout to stay well clear of memory limits.\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport torch\n\ndev = \"cuda\" if torch.cuda.is_available() else \"cpu\"\n_KEYS = (\"ts\", \"time_delta_input\", \"lead_time_delta\")   # the only keys the backbone reads\nmse = lambda a, b: torch.mean((a.float() - b.float()) ** 2).item()\n\ndef as_batch(sample):\n    \"\"\"One dataset sample -> a batch of 1 on the device, target separated out.\"\"\"\n    b = {k: torch.as_tensor(sample[k])[None].to(dev) for k in _KEYS}\n    tgt = torch.as_tensor(sample[\"forecast\"])[None][:, :, 0].to(dev)   # (1, 13, 4096, 4096)\n    return b, tgt\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------- A. the dataset's own flip\nds = train_data_loader.dataset\nsaved_p = ds.flip_probability\n\nds.flip_probability = 0.0\ns_up = ds[0]\nds.flip_probability = 1.0\ns_fl = ds[0]\n\nassert not s_up[\"was_flipped\"], \"p=0.0 still reported was_flipped=True\"\nassert s_fl[\"was_flipped\"], \"p=1.0 did not report was_flipped=True\"\nassert np.array_equal(s_fl[\"ts\"], np.flip(s_up[\"ts\"], axis=-2)), \\\\\n    \"input was not flipped north-south the way we expect\"\nassert np.array_equal(s_fl[\"forecast\"], np.flip(s_up[\"forecast\"], axis=-2)), \\\\\n    \"TARGET was not flipped with the input -- the model would be asked to predict a mirror image\"\nprint(\"A. flip fires, is recorded in was_flipped, and flips BOTH input and target. OK\\\\n\")\ndel s_up, s_fl\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------- B. paired scoring, both splits\nmodel = model.to(dev).eval()\nrows = []\nfor split, dl in ((\"train\", train_data_loader), (\"val\", val_data_loader)):\n    d = dl.dataset\n    d.flip_probability = 0.0            # we apply the flip ourselves, so the pairing is exact\n    for i in range(len(d)):\n        s = d[i]\n        b, tgt = as_batch(s)\n        for orient in (\"upright\", \"flipped\"):\n            bb, t = dict(b), tgt\n            if orient == \"flipped\":\n                bb[\"ts\"] = torch.flip(b[\"ts\"], dims=[-2])\n                t = torch.flip(tgt, dims=[-2])\n            with torch.no_grad(), torch.autocast(device_type=dev, dtype=torch.bfloat16):\n                pred = model(bb).float()\n            rows.append({\"split\": split, \"i\": i, \"time\": s[\"ds_index\"][:16],\n                         \"orientation\": orient, \"error\": mse(pred, t),\n                         \"persistence\": mse(bb[\"ts\"][:, :, -1], t)})\n            print(f\"\\\\r  B: {split} {i+1}/{len(d)} {orient:8s}\", end=\"\", flush=True)\n        del b, tgt, bb, t, pred\nprint()\n\ntab = pd.DataFrame(rows)\nprint(tab.to_string(index=False), \"\\\\n\")\nprint(tab.groupby([\"split\", \"orientation\"])[[\"error\", \"persistence\"]].mean().to_string(), \"\\\\n\")\n\n# Persistence never touches the model and a flip cannot change it, so these must agree exactly.\n_p = tab.pivot_table(index=[\"split\", \"i\"], columns=\"orientation\", values=\"persistence\")\n_gap = (_p[\"upright\"] - _p[\"flipped\"]).abs().max()\nassert _gap < 1e-4, f\"persistence differs by {_gap:.2e} between orientations -- flip is inconsistent\"\nprint(f\"persistence matches across orientations (max gap {_gap:.2e}). Table is trustworthy.\")\n\n_m = tab.pivot_table(index=[\"split\", \"i\"], columns=\"orientation\", values=\"error\")\n_delta = (_m[\"flipped\"] - _m[\"upright\"]).mean()\nprint(f\"mean error, flipped minus upright: {_delta:+.4f}\")\nprint(\"  ~0 -> orientation does not matter, so Hale's law cannot explain the cycle-25 failure.\")\nprint(\"  clearly positive -> the model is tied to the orientation it was pretrained on.\")\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------- C. flips reach the training loop\nmodel.train()\nopt = torch.optim.Adam([p for p in model.parameters() if p.requires_grad], lr=1e-4)\nfor d in (train_data_loader.dataset, val_data_loader.dataset):\n    d.flip_probability = saved_p if saved_p else 0.5\n\nprint(f\"\\\\nC. two batches through train_data_loader at flip_probability=\"\n      f\"{train_data_loader.dataset.flip_probability}:\")\nfor n, batch in enumerate(train_data_loader):\n    if n == 2:\n        break\n    flags = [bool(f) for f in batch[\"was_flipped\"]]\n    b = {k: batch[k][:1].to(dev) for k in _KEYS}            # model sees 1 sample; flags cover all\n    t = batch[\"forecast\"][:1][:, :, 0].to(dev)\n    with torch.autocast(device_type=dev, dtype=torch.bfloat16):\n        loss = torch.nn.functional.mse_loss(model(b).float(), t.float())\n    loss.backward()\n    opt.step()\n    opt.zero_grad(set_to_none=True)\n    print(f\"   batch {n}: was_flipped={flags}  loss={loss.item():.4f}  (stepped)\")\n    del b, t, loss\n\nprint(\"\\\\nFlips survive the dataset, the collate, and a real optimizer step.\")\nif dev == \"cuda\":\n    print(f\"peak GPU memory this cell: {torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated()/2**30:.1f} GB\")\n\ndel opt, rows\nmodel = model.to(\"cpu\").train()\nif dev == \"cuda\":\n    torch.cuda.empty_cache()\nprint(\"Model back on CPU. `tab` is kept so you can re-inspect it.\")\n'''\n\n# Anchor: insert straight after the forced-flip pass cell.\nassert \"Force one upright and one flipped pass\" in \"\".join(nb[\"cells\"][27][\"source\"])\nnb[\"cells\"][28:28] = [cell(\"markdown\", MD), cell(\"code\", CODE)]\n\njson.dump(nb, open(NB, \"w\"), indent=1)\n\nimport nbformat, warnings, ast\nwarnings.simplefilter(\"error\")\n_nb = nbformat.read(NB, as_version=4)\nnbformat.validate(_nb)\nast.parse(\"\".join(_nb.cells[29][\"source\"]))\nprint(f\"inserted at 28-29; notebook now {len(nb['cells'])} cells, valid, cell 29 parses\")\nPYEOF"
            },
            "raw_output": "inserted at 28-29; notebook now 58 cells, valid, cell 29 parses\nShell cwd was reset to /home/jovyan/tmp/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/flare_forecast/old-chats-notebooks",
            "permission_status": "resolved",
            "kind": "execute",
            "status": "completed",
            "selected_option_id": "allow-once",
            "tool_call_id": "toolu_bdrk_01ReHGzXMEi2QtrShvjeiNSa",
            "title": "cd surya_workshop && python - <<'PYEOF' import json, uuid NB = \"downstream_apps/flare_forecast/2_finetune_2D-image-flip.ipynb\" nb = json.load(open(NB)) def cell(kind, text): c = {\"cell_type\": kind, \"metadata\": {}, \"id\": uuid.uuid4().hex[:8], \"source\": text.strip(\"\\n\").splitlines(keepends=True)} if kind == \"code\": c[\"execution_count\"] = None c[\"outputs\"] = [] return c MD = \"\"\" ### The small check: flips through the real pipeline, before any long run Three questions, in order, on a handful of samples. **A. Does the dataset's flip fire, and does it flip the *target* too?** This is the one that matters most. If the input were flipped but the ground-truth image were not, you would be asking the model to predict a mirror image of what it was shown \u2014 guaranteed nonsense, and the loss would look bad for a reason that has nothing to do with Hale's law. Asserted directly. **B. Does orientation change the answer?** Each sample is scored **twice** \u2014 once upright, once flipped \u2014 with both its input and its target flipped together. Same flare, both orientations, so any difference is orientation and not \"which samples happened to get flipped.\" That pairing is what makes a 6-sample check readable; with random flipping you would need hundreds of samples to see the same thing. Random mixing is still the right thing for the real training runs \u2014 this is only how you measure precisely when N is tiny. Each row also carries the **persistence** error: what you'd score by just copying the last input frame forward. It doesn't involve the model, and flipping cannot change it, so upright and flipped persistence must come out **identical**. If they don't, the flip is being applied inconsistently somewhere and nothing else in the table can be trusted. **C. Do the flips actually reach the training loop?** Turns the random flipping back on, pulls real batches through `train_data_loader`, prints `was_flipped` per batch, and takes a real optimizer step. This is the end-to-end confirmation. Cost is roughly two forward passes per sample plus one read each, so it scales with `max_number_of_samples` in cell 11. Six samples is plenty. \"\"\" CODE = ''' # Small end-to-end flip check. Runs at batch size 1 throughout to stay well clear of memory limits. import numpy as np import pandas as pd import torch dev = \"cuda\" if torch.cuda.is_available() else \"cpu\" _KEYS = (\"ts\", \"time_delta_input\", \"lead_time_delta\") # the only keys the backbone reads mse = lambda a, b: torch.mean((a.float() - b.float()) ** 2).item() def as_batch(sample): \"\"\"One dataset sample -> a batch of 1 on the device, target separated out.\"\"\" b = {k: torch.as_tensor(sample[k])[None].to(dev) for k in _KEYS} tgt = torch.as_tensor(sample[\"forecast\"])[None][:, :, 0].to(dev) # (1, 13, 4096, 4096) return b, tgt # ---------------------------------------------------------------- A. the dataset's own flip ds = train_data_loader.dataset saved_p = ds.flip_probability ds.flip_probability = 0.0 s_up = ds[0] ds.flip_probability = 1.0 s_fl = ds[0] assert not s_up[\"was_flipped\"], \"p=0.0 still reported was_flipped=True\" assert s_fl[\"was_flipped\"], \"p=1.0 did not report was_flipped=True\" assert np.array_equal(s_fl[\"ts\"], np.flip(s_up[\"ts\"], axis=-2)), \\\\ \"input was not flipped north-south the way we expect\" assert np.array_equal(s_fl[\"forecast\"], np.flip(s_up[\"forecast\"], axis=-2)), \\\\ \"TARGET was not flipped with the input -- the model would be asked to predict a mirror image\" print(\"A. flip fires, is recorded in was_flipped, and flips BOTH input and target. OK\\\\n\") del s_up, s_fl # ---------------------------------------------------------------- B. paired scoring, both splits model = model.to(dev).eval() rows = [] for split, dl in ((\"train\", train_data_loader), (\"val\", val_data_loader)): d = dl.dataset d.flip_probability = 0.0 # we apply the flip ourselves, so the pairing is exact for i in range(len(d)): s = d[i] b, tgt = as_batch(s) for orient in (\"upright\", \"flipped\"): bb, t = dict(b), tgt if orient == \"flipped\": bb[\"ts\"] = torch.flip(b[\"ts\"], dims=[-2]) t = torch.flip(tgt, dims=[-2]) with torch.no_grad(), torch.autocast(device_type=dev, dtype=torch.bfloat16): pred = model(bb).float() rows.append({\"split\": split, \"i\": i, \"time\": s[\"ds_index\"][:16], \"orientation\": orient, \"error\": mse(pred, t), \"persistence\": mse(bb[\"ts\"][:, :, -1], t)}) print(f\"\\\\r B: {split} {i+1}/{len(d)} {orient:8s}\", end=\"\", flush=True) del b, tgt, bb, t, pred print() tab = pd.DataFrame(rows) print(tab.to_string(index=False), \"\\\\n\") print(tab.groupby([\"split\", \"orientation\"])[[\"error\", \"persistence\"]].mean().to_string(), \"\\\\n\") # Persistence never touches the model and a flip cannot change it, so these must agree exactly. _p = tab.pivot_table(index=[\"split\", \"i\"], columns=\"orientation\", values=\"persistence\") _gap = (_p[\"upright\"] - _p[\"flipped\"]).abs().max() assert _gap < 1e-4, f\"persistence differs by {_gap:.2e} between orientations -- flip is inconsistent\" print(f\"persistence matches across orientations (max gap {_gap:.2e}). Table is trustworthy.\") _m = tab.pivot_table(index=[\"split\", \"i\"], columns=\"orientation\", values=\"error\") _delta = (_m[\"flipped\"] - _m[\"upright\"]).mean() print(f\"mean error, flipped minus upright: {_delta:+.4f}\") print(\" ~0 -> orientation does not matter, so Hale's law cannot explain the cycle-25 failure.\") print(\" clearly positive -> the model is tied to the orientation it was pretrained on.\") # ---------------------------------------------------------------- C. flips reach the training loop model.train() opt = torch.optim.Adam([p for p in model.parameters() if p.requires_grad], lr=1e-4) for d in (train_data_loader.dataset, val_data_loader.dataset): d.flip_probability = saved_p if saved_p else 0.5 print(f\"\\\\nC. two batches through train_data_loader at flip_probability=\" f\"{train_data_loader.dataset.flip_probability}:\") for n, batch in enumerate(train_data_loader): if n == 2: break flags = [bool(f) for f in batch[\"was_flipped\"]] b = {k: batch[k][:1].to(dev) for k in _KEYS} # model sees 1 sample; flags cover all t = batch[\"forecast\"][:1][:, :, 0].to(dev) with torch.autocast(device_type=dev, dtype=torch.bfloat16): loss = torch.nn.functional.mse_loss(model(b).float(), t.float()) loss.backward() opt.step() opt.zero_grad(set_to_none=True) print(f\" batch {n}: was_flipped={flags} loss={loss.item():.4f} (stepped)\") del b, t, loss print(\"\\\\nFlips survive the dataset, the collate, and a real optimizer step.\") if dev == \"cuda\": print(f\"peak GPU memory this cell: {torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated()/2**30:.1f} GB\") del opt, rows model = model.to(\"cpu\").train() if dev == \"cuda\": torch.cuda.empty_cache() print(\"Model back on CPU. `tab` is kept so you can re-inspect it.\") ''' # Anchor: insert straight after the forced-flip pass cell. assert \"Force one upright and one flipped pass\" in \"\".join(nb[\"cells\"][27][\"source\"]) nb[\"cells\"][28:28] = [cell(\"markdown\", MD), cell(\"code\", CODE)] json.dump(nb, open(NB, \"w\"), indent=1) import nbformat, warnings, ast warnings.simplefilter(\"error\") _nb = nbformat.read(NB, as_version=4) nbformat.validate(_nb) ast.parse(\"\".join(_nb.cells[29][\"source\"])) print(f\"inserted at 28-29; notebook now {len(nb['cells'])} cells, valid, cell 29 parses\") PYEOF",
