      "body": "",
      "type": "msg",
      "id": "194fc339-896d-40b3-a616-b7ea1bd58067",
      "mentions": [],
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona"
    },
    {
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "time": 1789650818.3126302,
      "body": "Cells **28\u201329** are the check. Notebook is now 58 cells and validates.\n\n## What it proves, in order\n\n**A** \u2014 sets `flip_probability` to 0.0 then 1.0 on the live dataset object and reads sample 0 both ways. Asserts `was_flipped` tracks it, and asserts the **target** got flipped along with the input. That last one is the assertion I care about most: a flipped input with an unflipped target asks the model to predict a mirror image, which would tank the loss for a reason that has nothing to do with your hypothesis. Two reads, no model, so it's quick.\n\n**B** \u2014 scores every sample in both splits twice, upright and flipped, with input and target flipped together. Prints a per-sample table and a `groupby([\"split\",\"orientation\"])` summary, then the headline number: mean error flipped minus upright. Near zero means the model is blind to orientation and Hale's law can't explain the cycle-25 failure; clearly positive means it's tied to its pretraining orientation.\n\nIt also carries a persistence column (error from just copying the last input frame forward) and **asserts** that upright and flipped persistence match to 1e-4. Persistence never touches the model and a flip can't change it, so if those two disagree the flip is inconsistent somewhere and nothing else in the table means anything. That's the assertion that tells you the check itself is sound.\n\n**C** \u2014 turns random flipping back on, pulls real batches through `train_data_loader`, prints `was_flipped` per batch, and takes real optimizer steps. End-to-end confirmation that flips survive the dataset, the collate, and backprop.\n\nEverything runs at batch size 1 and the model goes back to the CPU at the end.\n\n## Before you run it\n\n**Bump `max_number_of_samples` in cell 11 from 3 to 6.** Two reasons: three samples gives you three paired comparisons, which is thin even for a check; and with `batch_size: 2` and `drop_last=True`, three samples is exactly one batch, so part C's two-batch loop only gets one iteration. Six gives you three batches and six pairs. Cost is two forward passes plus one read per sample \u2014 the speed check at cells 14\u201315 will have told you what a read costs.\n\nRun order: 0\u219229, skipping cell **31** (the slow CPU forward) as before. Cells 30 onward shifted by two; the trainer is now at 51 and `trainer.fit` at 53.\n\nOne thing this check deliberately doesn't tell you: whether the numbers are any good. At `157/159` fixed but zero fine-tuning, part B is scoring the pretrained model cold. If nothing beats persistence there, that's information about the 36-minute horizon, not a broken setup.",
      "raw_time": false,
      "type": "msg",
      "id": "e54a1f3e-6035-4c70-8833-ec46d1fce884",
      "mentions": []
    },
