      "raw_time": false,
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "body": "Confirmed from `builders.py:140-165`: `pin_memory=True` always, and when `num_workers > 0` it adds `multiprocessing_context=\"spawn\"` and `persistent_workers=True`. It never sets `prefetch_factor`, so it's PyTorch's default of 2.\n\n**For this check: longer, not faster.** Three reasons.\n\nParts A and B read `ds[i]` straight off the dataset object, bypassing the DataLoader entirely. `num_workers` has literally no effect on them. Only part C iterates `train_data_loader`.\n\nSpawn workers pay a fixed startup cost \u2014 each new process re-imports torch and rebuilds the dataset from scratch, tens of seconds. On a 6-sample check that's pure overhead you never amortize.\n\nAnd it breaks the runtime flip control. With `spawn`, each worker gets a pickled copy of the dataset made when it spawns. Setting `ds.flip_probability` in the notebook afterwards changes only the main process's copy, so whether part C's flip rate actually takes effect depends on whether some earlier cell already iterated the loader and spawned the persistent workers. That's an order-dependent, silent failure \u2014 exactly what you don't want in the cell whose job is confirming flips work.\n\n**For the real training run: it depends on what the speed check told you, and the ceiling is 2.**\n\nThe arithmetic on your ceiling: at `batch_size: 2`, `ts` is 3.25 GiB and `forecast` 1.62 GiB, so ~4.9 GiB per batch. Prefetch 2 means each worker holds up to two of those, ~9.8 GiB per worker, in **pinned** memory \u2014 which is non-swappable, so the kernel can't relieve pressure by paging it out. Four workers is ~39 GiB before the model, the optimizer, or anything else. That is the crash you already had: host RAM, SIGKILL, no traceback. Two workers \u2248 20 GiB keeps you well under 60.\n\nWhether even two helps: workers only buy you something if reading a sample is slow relative to a training step. Cells 14\u201315 give you the warm read time and cell 27 gives you the step time. If a warm read is much shorter than a step, the GPU is never waiting and extra workers buy nothing but RAM risk. If reads are still slow warm, 2 workers roughly halves the wait.\n\nOne thing to fix before any unattended run: `configs/config_script.yaml:158` says `num_workers: 8`. Cell 11 overrides it to 0, so the notebook is safe, but anything that reads the config directly \u2014 the training script \u2014 would try to spawn eight workers at ~9.8 GiB each. Drop that to 2.",
      "time": 1789650877.2888231,
      "type": "msg"
    },
    {
      "metadata": {
        "model": {
          "id": null,
          "settings": {}
        },
        "to_persona": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
        "settings": {}
      },
      "body": "im still confused: whats the best check / test i can do for just seeing how the model performs on flipped image data. the smallest fastest check i can do before we come up with a plan for trainin/validation/testing",
      "id": "252d071e-794f-4ae9-a049-cfabd7f69d8d",
      "sender": "jpereira",
      "time": 1789650968.4121583,
      "type": "msg",
      "raw_time": false
    },
    {
      "id": "bd3fa1b5-ae47-45f9-b229-2a5366f21f87",
      "mentions": [],
      "body": "The smallest useful check is: **take one solar image, score the pretrained model on it upright, then score it flipped, and compare the two errors. Two forward passes. No training at all.**\n\nThat's the whole thing. Your central question \u2014 does Surya handle both polarity orientations \u2014 is a question about the model as it comes out of the checkpoint. Fine-tuning is not part of the question, so you don't need any of it to get a first answer.\n\nThat's cell 29 part B. Let me make part C skippable so you can run only the minimum:",
      "raw_time": false,
      "time": 1789650996.338328,
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "type": "msg"
    },
    {
      "raw_time": false,
      "id": "3a0a7be2-6993-46bc-a061-b9b5e82cffc8",
      "body": "",
      "metadata": {
        "tool_calls": [
          {
            "status": "completed",
            "title": "cd surya_workshop && python - <<'PYEOF' import json NB = \"downstream_apps/flare_forecast/2_finetune_2D-image-flip.ipynb\" nb = json.load(open(NB)) src = nb[\"cells\"][29][\"source\"] assert \"Small end-to-end flip check\" in src[0], src[0] src.insert(1, \"# Set RUN_TRAINING_STEP = False for the minimum check: parts A and B only, no optimizer.\\n\") src.insert(2, \"RUN_TRAINING_STEP = True\\n\") i = next(k for k, l in enumerate(src) if l.startswith(\"# ------\") and \"C. flips reach\" in l) src.insert(i, 'if not RUN_TRAINING_STEP:\\n') src.insert(i + 1, ' model = model.to(\"cpu\").train()\\n') src.insert(i + 2, ' if dev == \"cuda\":\\n torch.cuda.empty_cache()\\n') src.insert(i + 3, ' raise SystemExit(\"Parts A and B done; part C skipped. `tab` holds the table.\")\\n') src.insert(i + 4, \"\\n\") json.dump(nb, open(NB, \"w\"), indent=1) import nbformat, warnings, ast warnings.simplefilter(\"error\") _nb = nbformat.read(NB, as_version=4) nbformat.validate(_nb) ast.parse(\"\".join(_nb.cells[29][\"source\"])) print(\"cell 29 now has a RUN_TRAINING_STEP toggle; notebook valid, cell parses\") PYEOF",
            "kind": "execute",
            "raw_input": {
              "command": "cd /home/jovyan/tmp/surya_workshop && /home/jovyan/envs/surya_ws/bin/python - <<'PYEOF'\nimport json\nNB = \"downstream_apps/flare_forecast/2_finetune_2D-image-flip.ipynb\"\nnb = json.load(open(NB))\nsrc = nb[\"cells\"][29][\"source\"]\nassert \"Small end-to-end flip check\" in src[0], src[0]\n\nsrc.insert(1, \"# Set RUN_TRAINING_STEP = False for the minimum check: parts A and B only, no optimizer.\\n\")\nsrc.insert(2, \"RUN_TRAINING_STEP = True\\n\")\n\ni = next(k for k, l in enumerate(src) if l.startswith(\"# ------\") and \"C. flips reach\" in l)\nsrc.insert(i, 'if not RUN_TRAINING_STEP:\\n')\nsrc.insert(i + 1, '    model = model.to(\"cpu\").train()\\n')\nsrc.insert(i + 2, '    if dev == \"cuda\":\\n        torch.cuda.empty_cache()\\n')\nsrc.insert(i + 3, '    raise SystemExit(\"Parts A and B done; part C skipped. `tab` holds the table.\")\\n')\nsrc.insert(i + 4, \"\\n\")\n\njson.dump(nb, open(NB, \"w\"), indent=1)\n\nimport nbformat, warnings, ast\nwarnings.simplefilter(\"error\")\n_nb = nbformat.read(NB, as_version=4)\nnbformat.validate(_nb)\nast.parse(\"\".join(_nb.cells[29][\"source\"]))\nprint(\"cell 29 now has a RUN_TRAINING_STEP toggle; notebook valid, cell parses\")\nPYEOF"
            },
            "tool_call_id": "toolu_bdrk_01C6e9bnadcZh6DH2VTcBhUf",
            "raw_output": "cell 29 now has a RUN_TRAINING_STEP toggle; notebook valid, cell parses\nShell cwd was reset to /home/jovyan/tmp/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/flare_forecast/old-chats-notebooks"
          }
