    {
      "raw_time": false,
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "type": "msg",
      "mentions": [],
      "time": 1789651023.5797212,
      "body": "## The minimum run\n\nSet `max_number_of_samples=2` in cell 11 and `RUN_TRAINING_STEP = False` at the top of cell 29. Then run only these:\n\n| cell | why |\n|---|---|\n| 2, 3 | env vars before torch, imports |\n| 5, 7 | config, scalers |\n| 10, 11 | build the datasets |\n| 17, 19 | build the model |\n| 21 | load the checkpoint |\n| **23** | **load the decoder \u2014 do not skip this** |\n| 29 | the check |\n\nSkip everything else, including 13, 15, 27, and 25.\n\n**Cell 23 is the one you can't skip.** Without it the output layer is random numbers, so both predictions are noise and the flipped-minus-upright difference is a comparison between two piles of fog. Nothing you measure would mean anything.\n\nCost: two file reads plus four forward passes. The reads dominate if the files aren't cached yet.\n\n## The two numbers\n\n```\npersistence matches across orientations (max gap ...)   <- if this assertion fails, stop; the check is broken\nmean error, flipped minus upright: +0.0000\n```\n\nNear zero: the pretrained model treats both orientations the same, so Hale's law can't be what's breaking cycle 25. Clearly positive: the model is tied to the orientation it was pretrained on, and your hypothesis survives.\n\nThe paired design is what makes two samples worth anything \u2014 it's the same flare scored both ways, so the difference can't be caused by one sample being an easier flare than another. That's the only reason a two-sample check is informative at all.\n\nIt is still two samples. It tells you the plumbing works and gives you a direction. Call it a signal, not a result; if the direction looks interesting, bump to 20 samples (still no training, still under an hour) before you plan the real runs around it.",
      "id": "2ff54c39-548a-4bb5-8bfd-bc83ce6ff849"
    },
    {
      "sender": "jpereira",
      "time": 1789651123.5444958,
      "id": "d8a8b7ab-0cb5-43bd-a144-fb188900c04e",
