    {
      "id": "c597b348-f7e5-4e20-aff7-b35218e8f425",
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "body": "Confirmed at `workshop_infrastructure/utils.py:404`:\n\n```python\nfor candidate in (k, f\"backbone.{k}\"):\n```\n\nTwo spellings, neither of which is `head_unembed.*`. So that function genuinely can't find the decoder, and line 411 prints its own tally \u2014 157 out of the 159 keys **it** matched. That message is a report from one step, not a final inventory.\n\nCell 23 then copies the remaining two right afterwards, and it asserts rather than warns, so if it ever stopped finding them you'd get a loud failure instead of a silent 157. The model ends up with all 159 \u2014 that's why your errors dropped from 0.94 to 0.22. The fix worked; the misleading message just stayed.\n\nSo nothing is broken. But you're right that leaving it is unpleasant, for a concrete reason: **cell 23 has to run after 21 and before 25**, and nothing enforces that. Run the cells out of order and you're silently back to a random decoder with only a `157 / 159` to hint at it.\n\nFixing it at the source is one line \u2014 add a third spelling:\n\n```python\nfor candidate in (k, f\"backbone.{k}\", f\"head_{k}\"):\n```\n\n`backbone` is still tried first so nothing that works today changes, and the match still requires the shape to agree, so it can't grab the wrong tensor. Cell 21 would then print `159 / 159`, the ordering hazard disappears, and cell 23 becomes a redundant-but-harmless verification you could keep or delete.\n\nThat's in shared infrastructure, not your notebook, and you told me notebook-only unless you say otherwise \u2014 so it's your call. It would also fix this for the 1D template and anyone else who forks it, since the bug is generic to the `head_` convention that `apply_peft_lora` depends on. Want me to make it?",
      "mentions": [],
      "raw_time": false,
      "time": 1789651999.910153,
      "type": "msg"
    },
