      "id": "c8b51966-65a8-4c16-9f02-b22a2c7757da",
      "body": "Looked at the dependency chain. Short answer: **skip cell 31 and the metrics demos. You need five cells to reach the images, and you don't need the trainer at all.**\n\nBoth plotting cells (55 and 56) call `lit_model`, not `model`. `lit_model` is created in cell 47. So the chain is:\n\n| run | why |\n|---|---|\n| 33, 34 | the four `FlareMetrics` objects that cell 47 needs |\n| 42, 43 | `Flare2DLightningModule` |\n| 45 | `seed_everything` \u2014 cheap, keeps things reproducible |\n| **47** | builds `lit_model` from `model` + `metrics` |\n| 55 and/or 56 | the images |\n\n**Skip 31.** That's `output = model.forward(batch)` on the **CPU** \u2014 a 366M-param model over 4096\u00b2 inputs with no GPU. It's the slowest cell in the notebook and nothing you need depends on it. Cells 36, 38, 40 are demos that consume its `output`, so they go too.\n\n**Skip 49, 51, 53** unless you actually want to train. Loggers, trainer, and `trainer.fit` are not needed to plot \u2014 `lit_model` is fully usable the moment cell 47 runs.\n\n## Plotting before you train is the point\n\nThat's the gate from the plan: run cell 55 or 56 on the untrained model and see whether the prediction looks like the Sun. It should \u2014 LoRA adapters initialize to zero contribution, so `lit_model` right now is functionally the pretrained checkpoint, which we just measured at 0.22 error against 0.43 for persistence. If you get fog instead, cell 23 didn't take.\n\nCell 56 also prints the persistence comparison itself:\n\n```\n  model              : ...\n  persistence        : ...\n  skill vs persist.  : ...%   (>0 = model helps)\n```\n\nwhich should roughly agree with the +49% you'd get from the table I ran.\n\n## Your flip image check is already in cell 56\n\nNear the bottom of its provenance block it prints:\n\n```\nFlipped?  ->  flare_dataset = ...\n```\n\nSo: run cell 56 once with `train_data_loader.dataset.flip_probability = 0.0`, then again with `1.0`, and you get the same flare rendered both ways with its own RMSE and skill numbers. That's the visual version of the table \u2014 and it's the fastest way to see whether a flipped prediction looks *worse* or just *upside down*.\n\nOne thing to know: cell 56 has `STRIDE = 1`, so it transforms full 4096\u00d74096 frames. Fine on your 118 GB of RAM, but set `STRIDE = 4` if you want the figure ~16\u00d7 faster while you're iterating.",
      "time": 1789652228.4101686,
      "raw_time": false
    },
