      "type": "msg",
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "mentions": [],
      "body": "Cell 29 part B deliberately sets `flip_probability = 0.0` on **both** datasets \u2014 it applies the flip itself so the pairing is exact. The restore only happens in part C. You ran with `RUN_TRAINING_STEP = False`, which exits before the restore, so both datasets were left at 0.0. Cell 56 then read an unflipped sample. My bug.\n\n**Right now**, to see a flipped prediction, run this before cell 56:\n\n```python\ntrain_data_loader.dataset.flip_probability = 1.0\n```\n\nThen re-run 56 and check the `Flipped?  ->  flare_dataset = True` line to confirm.\n\nFixing it properly in cell 29 so it always restores:",
      "id": "66510e2b-2135-47cd-9e05-b5270d8a3142",
      "time": 1789653494.246027,
      "raw_time": false
    },
    {
      "id": "90ca67d1-9b05-440e-a3a5-a2e0aee63de3",
      "type": "msg",
      "metadata": {
        "tool_calls": [
          {
            "title": "cd surya_workshop && python - <<'PYEOF' import json NB = \"downstream_apps/flare_forecast/2_finetune_2D-image-flip.ipynb\" nb = json.load(open(NB)) src = nb[\"cells\"][29][\"source\"] # 1. Restore flip_probability on both datasets as soon as part B's paired scoring is done. old = 'tab = pd.DataFrame(rows)\\n' i = src.index(old) src[i:i] = [ \"# Part B borrowed both datasets by zeroing flip_probability; give it back immediately,\\n\", \"# so anything run afterwards (the plotting cells especially) sees the flips again.\\n\", \"for d in (train_data_loader.dataset, val_data_loader.dataset):\\n\", \" d.flip_probability = saved_p\\n\", f\"print(f\\\"flip_probability restored to {{saved_p}} on both datasets\\\")\\n\", \"\\n\", ] # 2. The early-exit path no longer needs its own restore, but make it explicit and loud. j = src.index('if not RUN_TRAINING_STEP:\\n') assert src[j + 3].startswith(' raise SystemExit') src[j + 3] = (' raise SystemExit(\"Parts A and B done; part C skipped. `tab` holds the table, \"\\n' ' f\"and flip_probability is back at {saved_p}.\")\\n') json.dump(nb, open(NB, \"w\"), indent=1) import nbformat, warnings, ast warnings.simplefilter(\"error\") _nb = nbformat.read(NB, as_version=4) nbformat.validate(_nb) ast.parse(\"\".join(_nb.cells[29][\"source\"])) print(\"cell 29 patched; notebook valid and parses\") PYEOF",
