    {
      "raw_time": false,
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "mentions": [],
      "id": "185fbdcd-8e23-49f1-96cd-17b5f72924e7",
      "body": "Killed the background smoke test I had running \u2014 it was holding GPU memory and you need the card.\n\n## Why the plot comes before training\n\nIt doesn't skip the model \u2014 it *runs* the model, it just doesn't *train* it first. Inside the plot cell there's a block:\n\n```\n# 0. RUN THE MODEL\nlit_model = lit_model.to(\"cuda\").eval()\n...\npred_norm = lit_model(_b)[0].float().cpu().numpy()\n```\n\nSo there's a real forward pass. What's missing at that point is `trainer.fit`, which is cell 15.\n\nThat ordering is deliberate, for two reasons. LoRA adapters initialize so they contribute nothing, so before fitting `lit_model` *is* the pretrained checkpoint \u2014 which makes that plot your untrained baseline, the thing you compare a fine-tuned result against. And it's the gate for the decoder fix: if the prediction looks like the Sun, cell 6 worked; if it's fog, it didn't. Catching that before spending hours training is the point.\n\nAfter you run cell 15, just re-run cell 13 and you get the same figure from the trained model.\n\n## Yes, and the check is now in the notebook\n\n`1.0` flips every sample, `0.0` flips none, `0.5` rolls an independent coin on **every read** \u2014 so a mixed run really does contain both.\n\nI already made the two edits for this:\n\n**Cell 12** reads the *same* flare twice from the dataset, once with the flip forced off and once forced on, then collates both into **one batch of 2** and runs it through `lit_model` with a real backward pass. It asserts the batch is genuinely mixed (`was_flipped = [False, True]`), prints each orientation's error separately, and prints peak GPU memory. That's the confirmation that training and validation can handle both at once, not just one at a time.\n\n**Cell 13** now loops over both of those samples, so you get one figure set labeled `UPRIGHT` and one labeled `FLIPPED` from a single run. Both come from the dataset's own flip, so it's the real pipeline being tested, not arrays I flipped by hand. I also set `STRIDE = 4` there since it's now four figures instead of two.\n\n`PLOT_IDX` at the top of cell 12 picks which flare, and cell 13 uses the same value.\n\nOne caveat: cell 12 does a backward pass at batch size 2, which is the heaviest thing in the notebook. It has a `BACKWARD = True` toggle \u2014 set it `False` if it doesn't fit. I hadn't finished verifying that it does before I stopped the job; the peak-memory line it prints will tell you.",
      "type": "msg",
      "time": 1789654752.2600863
    },
    {
      "raw_time": false,
      "sender": "jpereira",
      "id": "4d3a2573-f89a-44fe-b970-84bce3a3fde2",
      "body": "no i think you misunderstood. \n- i need the plots AFTER the model runs so i can check what the predicted image is VS ground truth.\n- i need the image stack flips to be random yes (50% either way), but i dont want the same data copied and flipped. i need to plot an example of both an event that was flipped and event that wasn't, and that'll be the check whether its working for both or just one or just the other etc. does this make sense? i dont want to force the model to work if it isn't meant to, i just need to check to see whether this process is being implemented properly. \n- what does batch size do? once this is done, how do i know how large the training/val/test stuff is right now? i want to know this last since i want to run the code checks first before i come up with a better strategy before the presentation im giving tomorrow",
      "type": "msg",
      "time": 1789655051.0004003,
      "metadata": {
        "settings": {
          "__mode__": "auto"
        },
        "to_persona": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
