    {
      "time": 1789655425.821954,
      "type": "msg",
      "mentions": [],
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "raw_time": false,
      "body": "`2b_flip_minimal.ipynb` is reworked. What changed:\n\n**Plots now run after training.** Cell 13 is `trainer.fit`; cells 14\u201315 are the check. So the prediction you see comes from the trained model, compared against ground truth, as you asked.\n\n**No more copied-and-flipped sample.** I deleted that cell. Cell 14 leaves `flip_probability` at 0.5 and just *watches*: it reads samples one at a time, records what `was_flipped` actually came back as, and keeps the first one of each kind. It prints the coin sequence as it goes (`....F` = four upright then a flipped one) and names the two events it picked. Cell 15 plots both, with an assert that each figure's label matches that sample's own recorded orientation.\n\nSo the two figures are **two different flares** that landed on opposite sides of the coin. Nothing is forced. If only one orientation ever turns up, cell 14 stops with an error saying so \u2014 that failure *is* the finding.\n\nI bumped `N_SAMPLES` from 2 to 6, because two coin flips give you both orientations only half the time. At 6 it's ~97%. I verified the scan logic over 6 random seeds without touching the GPU \u2014 both orientations found every time, always from different events.\n\n---\n\n**What `batch_size` does.** It's how many flares go through the model together before it updates its weights once. Three consequences:\n\n- **Memory.** Everything scales linearly. One sample is ~2.6 GB in RAM and peaked at 11.4 GB on the GPU. `batch_size: 2` doubles both.\n- **Update quality.** The weight update is the *average* correction across the batch. Bigger batch = smoother, less jumpy learning; smaller batch = noisier steps.\n- **How many updates you get.** With 6,279 training flares at `batch_size: 2` you get 3,139 updates per epoch. At 4, you'd get 1,570 \u2014 half as many updates, each on twice the evidence.\n\nYour host-RAM crash earlier was batch size \u00d7 workers \u00d7 prefetch, so 2 is the right ceiling here.\n\n**How big the data actually is** (cell 16 measures this, uncapped \u2014 it only matches indices, no image reads, ~8 s):\n\n| split | flares | span |\n|---|---|---|\n| train | **6,279** | 2010-06-12 \u2192 2021-12-31 |\n| val | **321** | 2011-01-21 \u2192 2023-01-30 |\n\nTrain by year: 2011: 1136, 2013: 1250, **2014: 1589, 2015: 1199**, 2016: 186, 2017: 264, 2021: 448, rest small. Val: **2014: 77, 2015: 77, 2023: 107**, rest small.\n\nTwo things worth knowing before you plan tomorrow:\n\n1. **There is no test split.** `build_helio_dataloaders` returns train and val only. So \"train/val/test\" is currently train/val. If you want a held-out test set you'd carve it out of those 321 val flares.\n2. **107 of your 321 validation flares are from 2023 \u2014 real cycle 25** \u2014 while training is cycle 24. That's the actual experiment sitting in your data for free, alongside the simulated flip.\n\nYour current run only sees 6 of those 6,279, because `N_SAMPLES` caps it. That's correct for a plumbing check; cell 16 tells you what you'd be scaling up to.",
      "id": "5b3b8f43-ca36-4392-85b1-d8966946c8f3"
    },
