        }
      },
      "time": 1789658023.1221216
    },
    {
      "mentions": [],
      "body": "Two answers, because there's a cap in the way.\n\n**As the notebook is configured right now: 6 training samples and 6 validation samples.** Cell 4 sets `N_SAMPLES = 6` and passes it as `max_number_of_samples`, which caps both splits. (I raised it from 2 to 6 so the random flip has a good chance of producing both orientations.) At `batch_size: 2` that's 3 training steps and 3 validation steps per epoch. The YAML's `max_samples: 10` is inert here \u2014 the notebook's `N_SAMPLES` overrides it.\n\n**What's actually available if you remove the cap: 6,279 training and 321 validation flares.** Measured, not estimated:\n\n| split | flares | span |\n|---|---|---|\n| train | 6,279 | 2010-06-12 \u2192 2021-12-31 |\n| val | 321 | 2011-01-21 \u2192 2023-01-30 |\n\nTrain by year: 2010: 88, 2011: 1136, 2013: 1250, **2014: 1589, 2015: 1199**, 2016: 186, 2017: 264, 2018: 7, 2019: 26, 2020: 86, 2021: 448.\nVal by year: 2011: 14, 2013: 11, **2014: 77, 2015: 77**, 2016: 21, 2017: 7, 2019: 5, 2021: 2, **2023: 107**.\n\nFull-set steps per epoch at `batch_size: 2`: 3,139 train, 160 val.\n\nThree things that follow:\n\n- **There is no test set.** `build_helio_dataloaders` returns train and val only. Your \"train/val/test\" is currently train/val.\n- **The cap takes the *earliest* flares, not a random sample.** `flare_dataset.py` slices `[:N]` off a list sorted by peak time, so your 6 samples are all from June 2010 \u2014 deep solar minimum. That's fine for a plumbing check but it's the least informative possible data for a polarity hypothesis. If you scale up, take a random or year-stratified subset instead of the first N.\n- **107 of your 321 validation flares are from 2023 \u2014 real cycle 25**, against cycle-24-only training. The real experiment is already in your data next to the simulated flip.\n\nCell 16 prints all of this at the end of the notebook so you can quote it live.",
