    {
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "time": 1789658560.5308404,
      "type": "msg",
      "id": "efb466b4-0bac-4261-a384-4fb3ce87e2af",
      "mentions": [],
      "raw_time": false,
      "body": "Here's the per-year range. The answer has a real surprise in it.\n\n**TRAIN \u2014 6,279 samples, always March\u2192December, never January or February**\n\n| year | samples | first | last | distinct days |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2010 | 88 | Jun 12 | Dec 31 | 49 |\n| 2011 | 1,136 | Mar 02 | Dec 31 | 228 |\n| 2013 | 1,250 | Mar 02 | Dec 31 | 235 |\n| 2014 | 1,589 | Mar 02 | Dec 31 | 281 |\n| 2015 | 1,199 | Mar 02 | Dec 31 | 226 |\n| 2016 | 186 | Mar 04 | Dec 10 | 79 |\n| 2017 | 264 | Mar 26 | Oct 20 | 66 |\n| 2018 | 7 | Mar 02 | Jul 06 | 7 |\n| 2019 | 26 | Mar 08 | May 15 | 10 |\n| 2020 | 86 | May 29 | Dec 14 | 33 |\n| 2021 | 448 | Mar 03 | Dec 31 | 115 |\n\n**VAL \u2014 321 samples, and every single one falls in Jan 16\u201330**\n\n| year | samples | first | last | distinct days |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2011 | 14 | Jan 21 | Jan 28 | 5 |\n| 2013 | 11 | Jan 16 | Jan 30 | 6 |\n| 2014 | 77 | Jan 16 | Jan 30 | 14 |\n| 2015 | 77 | Jan 16 | Jan 30 | 14 |\n| 2016 | 21 | Jan 20 | Jan 30 | 9 |\n| 2017 | 7 | Jan 21 | Jan 28 | 2 |\n| 2019 | 5 | Jan 26 | Jan 30 | 3 |\n| 2021 | 2 | Jan 19 | Jan 20 | 2 |\n| 2023 | 107 | Jan 16 | Jan 30 | 15 |\n\nThree things you should know before tomorrow:\n\n**1. The split is a calendar holdout, not a random one.** Validation is a fixed ~2-week window in mid-to-late January of each year; training is March through December. There is zero date overlap, which is good \u2014 no leakage \u2014 but it means \"validation performance\" specifically means \"performance on late-January solar conditions.\" Every val year is only 15 days wide, so a handful of active regions produce most of the samples in each year. Treat the per-year val numbers as small-sample.\n\n**2. 2012 and 2022 are absent from both splits entirely.** Not a filtering artifact of the flare matching \u2014 those years just aren't in your index CSVs. Worth confirming that's intentional, since 2012 is near cycle-24 max and would otherwise be prime training data.\n\n**3. Your cycle-25 comparison is 15 days versus years.** The 107 \"2023\" samples all come from Jan 16\u201330, 2023 \u2014 a single 15-day stretch, so probably only a few distinct active regions. Comparing them against 2014\u201315 (154 samples, also all late-January) is a legitimate test, but the 2023 side is effectively one epoch of solar weather. Say that out loud rather than let someone ask it.\n\nThat last point strengthens the case for the flip experiment as your primary evidence: it's the only version of the polarity question where you have thousands of samples on both sides instead of two weeks."
    },
