    },
    {
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "mentions": [],
      "id": "74c66039-9858-43c9-a2f3-210f1b7020df",
      "time": 1789660079.1045566,
      "raw_time": false,
      "body": "Sorry \u2014 I was measuring instead of answering. Here are the numbers that decide everything, and the plan.\n\n## What I measured\n\n| quantity | measured |\n|---|---|\n| read one sample **already on local disk** | **22.4 s** (single process, CPU-bound: netCDF decode + normalize) |\n| read one sample **cold from S3** | **63 s** (\u224840 s download + 22 s decode) |\n| data per sample | 1.64 GB (3 frames \u00d7 560 MB) |\n| GPU forward, batch 1 | 0.6 s |\n| samples already fully cached | **23 of 6,600** \u2014 the 2.8 TB cache is from runs with different time offsets |\n| machine | 16 vCPU, 71 GB RAM free, L40S 44 GB (9 GB held by 2 stale processes \u2014 kill those) |\n\n**Your bottleneck is not the GPU, it's reading data \u2014 by a factor of ~35.** The GPU spends 0.6 s and then waits 22 s. Every design decision below follows from that one fact.\n\nStill measuring: whether downloads parallelize across workers. I'll report it when it lands; it affects the download estimate, not the structure.\n\n## The controls\n\n| knob | set it to | why |\n|---|---|---|\n| `batch_size` | **1** | You're data-bound, so batch 2 costs the same wall time per sample but halves your number of weight updates. Batch 1 also frees RAM for more workers. |\n| `num_workers` | **4** | 4 workers \u00d7 2 prefetched \u00d7 1 sample \u00d7 2.6 GB \u2248 21 GB, ~30 GB with pinned copies. Under your 60 GB ceiling. This is what turns 22.4 s/sample into ~5.6 s/sample. 8 workers would be faster but is what crashed you before. |\n| epochs | **don't set it \u2014 set `max_steps: 500`** | An epoch is one pass over your training set, so \"2 epochs\" means something different for 40 samples than for 320. Fixing *steps* instead means every run gets the same amount of optimization and the only thing that varies is how much unique data fed it. That's the whole point of a data-scaling experiment. Also set `max_time={\"hours\": 1.5}` as a crash guard. |\n| validation during training | **off** | Validating costs more than training here. Do one explicit evaluation pass after `fit`. |\n| checkpoint selection | **none \u2014 use the final weights** | With a fixed step budget this is the honest choice, it removes a confound, and it saves hours of val passes. |\n| `flip_probability` | **0.5 for training** | Natural mixing, as you set up. |\n| evaluation flips | **score each val sample twice, upright and flipped** | Different from the plot check. For *scoring*, the same-sample-both-ways design removes sample-to-sample variance, which is what lets you see a small effect with 80 samples instead of needing 800. It's a paired comparison, not forcing the model to succeed. |\n| LoRA `target_modules` | **fix to `[fc1, fc2, attn.qkv, attn.proj]`** | Your YAML has 4 of 6 names that don't exist in Surya, so attention is currently never adapted. |\n\n## Train / val / test\n\nUse the calendar structure that's already in your data rather than an arbitrary split:\n\n- **train** \u2014 320 samples drawn from the 6,279, year-stratified across 2011/2013/2014/2015\n- **val** \u2014 80 samples from the 2014\u201315 validation flares (same cycle as training)\n- **test** \u2014 all 107 samples from **Jan 2023, real cycle 25**, touched exactly once at the very end\n\nThat makes \"test\" scientifically meaningful \u2014 it *is* the cross-cycle generalization question \u2014 instead of a random holdout.\n\nOne thing to fix: `max_number_of_samples` takes the **earliest** N samples, so your training set would be all June 2010 solar minimum. You need an explicit year-stratified sample list instead.\n\n## Four runs answer all three of your advisor's suggestions\n\n| run | training data | steps | serves |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **R0** | none (pretrained Surya) | 0 | *baseline vs finetuned, identical val* \u2014 **and it's free** |\n| **R1** | 40 samples (subset A\u2081 \u2282 A) | 500 | data-amount scaling |\n| **R2** | 160 samples (subset A) | 500 | scaling + ensemble member 1 |\n| **R3** | 160 samples (subset B, disjoint from A) | 500 | *different training set, same val* \u2014 ensemble member 2 |\n| **R4** | 320 samples (A \u222a B) | 500 | scaling, top end |\n\nR1/R2/R4 give the scaling curve. R2 vs R3 gives the ensemble comparison at matched size. R0 vs everything gives baseline vs finetuned. And since every run reports its results split by orientation, **your flip question comes out of the same table for free** \u2014 no separate experiment.\n\nAlways report `error_persistence` (copy the last input frame forward) next to every number. At a 36-minute horizon it's a strong competitor and your numbers are meaningless without it.\n\n## Timeline\n\n**Now \u2192 +1 h.** Fix `target_modules`. Write the stratified sample-list cell and freeze the exact lists to CSV so all runs share them. Kill the stale GPU processes.\n\n**+1 h \u2192 +3 h. Run R0.** Evaluation only, no training: pretrained Surya on val (80) and test (107), each scored both orientations. This warms the cache for every later run *and* gives you a presentable headline result tonight with zero training. If nothing else finishes, you still have a talk.\n\n**Evening \u2192 overnight, ~6 h unattended.** Launch R1\u2192R2\u2192R3\u2192R4 as a chain under `nohup` with the per-run time cap, so a crash in one doesn't cost the rest. Estimated ~5.8 h including the one-time downloads of the 320-sample pool.\n\n**Morning, ~2 h.** Combine tables, make the scaling plot and the orientation comparison, plus your input/truth/prediction figure for one M-class flare.\n\n## Two things to say out loud in the talk\n\n500 LoRA steps on a 366M-parameter model is a *small* intervention. Present these as **comparisons between conditions**, not as an attempt to build a good forecaster \u2014 the framing is \"does more data / different data / finetuning at all move the needle,\" which is exactly what your advisor asked for.\n\nAnd MSE training pushes predictions toward the average likely image, which blurs away small bright features \u2014 i.e. flares. A blurry flare-free prediction is partly the loss function, not a bug. That's the argument for adding a flare-region-masked error to your table.\n\nWant me to write the cells for this \u2014 the stratified sample lists, the R0 evaluation pass, and the run chain?",
      "type": "msg"
    },
    {
      "type": "msg",
      "time": 1789660360.5852227,
      "metadata": {
        "to_persona": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
        "settings": {
          "__mode__": "auto"
        },
        "model": {
          "id": null,
          "settings": {}
        }
      },
      "raw_time": false,
      "body": "so 500 samples is what you're saying? or what",
      "id": "6ecb8634-5404-4667-8b59-06d3e4c577a8",
      "sender": "jpereira"
    },
    {
      "raw_time": false,
      "body": "Two different 500s got tangled together \u2014 my fault for reusing the number.\n\n**Unique flares you download: 507.** That's the data you actually pull from S3, once, shared by every run:\n\n| split | flares | purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| train pool | 320 | all four runs draw from this |\n| val | 80 | from 2014\u201315, used for every comparison |\n| test | 107 | all of Jan 2023, cycle 25, touched once at the end |\n\n**500 = weight updates per run, not samples.** At `batch_size: 1`, one update sees one flare, so a run reads 500 sample-*views* \u2014 but it revisits the same flares repeatedly. That's what an epoch is:\n\n| run | unique flares it trains on | updates | passes over its data (epochs) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| R1 | 40 | 500 | 12.5 |\n| R2 | 160 | 500 | 3.1 |\n| R3 | 160 (different 160) | 500 | 3.1 |\n| R4 | 320 | 500 | 1.6 |\n\nEvery run gets the same 500 updates. The only thing that changes is how much unique data feeds them \u2014 which is exactly the \"different amounts of training data, same validation\" experiment.\n\nWhy the two numbers landed so close: 320 + 80 + 107 = 507 unique flares to download, and 500 updates per run. Coincidence.\n\n**Cost, so you can sanity-check it:**\n\n- Downloads: 507 \u00d7 1.64 GB \u2248 **830 GB**, one time, at 63 s per sample cold. With 4 workers that's roughly 2.2 hours, spread across the runs rather than paid upfront.\n- Training: 4 runs \u00d7 500 views \u00d7 ~5.6 s \u2248 **3.1 h**\n- Evaluations: 5 val passes (80 flares \u00d7 2 orientations) + 2 test passes \u2248 **1.9 h**\n- Total \u2248 **5.8 h of machine time**, which fits the overnight window with margin.\n\nIf that feels like too much data to babysit, the knob to turn is the **train pool**: dropping 320 \u2192 160 (so runs become 40 / 80 / 80 / 160) cuts downloads by half and costs you the top of the scaling curve. The val and test sets should not shrink \u2014 they're what every comparison rests on, and 80 is already small.",
      "id": "58e8b9f6-0428-481c-95c0-adf0442d276d",
      "time": 1789660378.88502,
      "type": "msg",
      "mentions": [],
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona"
    },
    {
      "id": "0a548570-b3df-4f81-8c95-262afdbefa9f",
      "raw_time": false,
      "sender": "jpereira",
      "body": "ok. as a TEST for how long it SHOULD take: what would numbers be if i want the whole code to run for like 1 hr (if it actually takes 1hr then i can estimate that the code will actually take about 6 hrs rigt?)",
      "type": "msg",
      "time": 1789660481.779929,
      "metadata": {
        "settings": {
          "__mode__": "auto"
        },
        "to_persona": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
