    },
    {
      "type": "msg",
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "mentions": [],
      "time": 1789660541.820107,
      "raw_time": false,
      "body": "Yes, \u00d76 is the right instinct \u2014 with one correction, because fixed overheads don't scale.\n\n## The 1/6-scale dry run\n\nKeep the structure identical (same 5 runs, same code path), divide every size by 6:\n\n| knob | full plan | dry run |\n|---|---|---|\n| train pool | 320 | **52** |\n| R1 / R2 / R3 / R4 train sizes | 40 / 160 / 160 / 320 | **7 / 26 / 26 / 52** |\n| val | 80 | **14** |\n| test | 107 | **18** |\n| `max_steps` per run | 500 | **84** |\n| `batch_size` | 1 | 1 |\n| `num_workers` | 4 | 4 |\n| unique flares to download | 507 | **84** |\n\n**Predicted dry-run wall time: 56 minutes.** Here's the arithmetic so you can check it rather than trust it:\n\n- Total sample reads = 4 runs \u00d7 84 train views + 5 val passes \u00d7 14 + 2 test passes \u00d7 18 = **442 reads**\n- Of those, 84 are first-touch (cold, 63 s) and 358 are repeats (warm, 22.4 s)\n- Serial: 84\u00d763 + 358\u00d722.4 = 13,300 s \u2192 **\u00f7 4 workers = 56 min**\n\nThe same arithmetic on the full plan gives 2,614 reads (507 cold, 2,107 warm) = 22 h serial \u00f7 4 = **5.5 h**, which is where my 5.8 h came from.\n\n## The correction to \u00d76\n\nEach run pays ~2 minutes of fixed setup \u2014 building the 366M model, loading the checkpoint, applying LoRA, Lightning init. Across 5 runs that's ~10 minutes, which is **18% of a 56-minute dry run but only 3% of a 5.5-hour real run**. So a naive \u00d76 inflates the estimate. Use:\n\n```\nfull \u2248 6 \u00d7 (dry_run_minutes \u2212 10) + 10\n```\n\n- Dry run lands at **56 min** \u2192 full \u2248 5.6 h \u2192 plan is sound, go as designed.\n- Dry run lands at **90 min** \u2192 full \u2248 8.3 h \u2192 cut the train pool to 160 (runs become 20/80/80/160) and you're back to ~5 h.\n- Dry run lands at **35 min** \u2192 full \u2248 3.5 h \u2192 you have room to raise `max_steps` to 800, which is the better use of spare budget than more data.\n\n## Two things that make the dry run worth more\n\n**Draw the dry-run samples as a subset of the real pools**, not from elsewhere. Then the 84 flares you download stay warm for the overnight run \u2014 nothing is downloaded twice, and the real run comes in slightly *under* the estimate.\n\n**You'll know the timing in five minutes, not an hour.** The number that decides everything is the steady-state seconds-per-step on the Lightning progress bar once the cache is warm. Expect **~5.6 s/it (\u22480.18 it/s)** at `batch_size: 1` with 4 workers. If you see that, the plan holds. If you see 12 s/it, halve the pool before you commit to the night.\n\nSo the real purpose of the full hour is different from timing: it's proving the **chain runs unattended without crashing** \u2014 four runs back to back, checkpoints written, tables saved, no OOM on run 3. That's the thing you can't learn in five minutes, and it's the thing that would otherwise cost you the whole night.\n\nOne item first, though: two stale processes are holding 9 GB of your 44 GB GPU. Kill them before the dry run or run 3 may OOM for a reason that has nothing to do with your code.",
      "id": "c7b06821-4269-4cd1-b58d-078e54eb8055"
    },
    {
      "time": 1789660769.153134,
      "raw_time": false,
      "body": "number of workers being 4: why? why 4 runs with different numbers of unique flares for training? im confused...",
      "type": "msg",
      "id": "db873a61-c3e9-45f4-87d0-4322f5d09e20",
      "metadata": {
        "settings": {
          "__mode__": "auto"
        },
        "to_persona": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
        "model": {
          "settings": {},
          "id": null
        }
      },
      "sender": "jpereira"
    },
    {
      "type": "msg",
      "raw_time": false,
      "id": "15e09a2a-f926-4c29-b33f-544d4bfe4636",
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "mentions": [],
      "time": 1789660806.5135734,
      "body": "Two separate things. Taking them in order.\n\n## Why `num_workers: 4`\n\nA worker is a **separate process that reads and normalizes samples while the GPU is busy**. Reading one sample takes 22.4 s of pure CPU work (decompress three 560 MB netCDF files, apply signum-log, apply z-score). The GPU step takes 0.6 s. With `num_workers: 0` the GPU sits idle for 22 s between every step \u2014 that's the flat WandB utilization trace you saw.\n\nFour workers read four samples at once, so the effective cost drops to 22.4 / 4 \u2248 **5.6 s per sample**. More workers would be faster still. The limit is RAM, and here's the arithmetic:\n\n- one sample = `ts` (13\u00d72\u00d74096\u00d74096 float32 = 1.74 GB) + `forecast` (0.87 GB) = **2.6 GB**\n- each worker holds `prefetch_factor` (2, PyTorch default) \u00d7 `batch_size` samples in flight\n- so at `batch_size: 1`: 4 workers \u00d7 2 \u00d7 2.6 GB \u2248 **21 GB**, plus roughly another copy for `pin_memory` staging \u2192 **~30 GB**\n\nThat's comfortably inside your \"under and not close to 60 GB\" rule. For comparison, **your earlier crash was 4 workers at `batch_size: 2`** \u2014 same worker count, twice the samples each: 4 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 2.6 \u2248 42 GB plus pinning \u2248 80 GB. Host RAM, SIGKILL, no traceback. Dropping to `batch_size: 1` is what makes 4 workers safe again.\n\nYou have 16 vCPU (8 physical cores) and 71 GB free, so **6 workers is also viable** \u2014 ~3.7 s/sample, a 33% speedup, at ~45 GB. That's under 60 but not comfortably. Worth trying during the dry run with `free -g` in another terminal; if it holds, take the speedup. 8 would saturate your cores and put you back near the crash.\n\n## Why four runs with different amounts of data\n\nBecause that's the experiment your advisor asked for \u2014 two of the three, actually, and they share the same downloads.\n\nThe 320-flare pool is split so the runs nest:\n\n```\npool of 320 unique flares\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 A  = 160  \u2500\u252c\u2500 A\u2081 = 40\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 B  = 160  \u2500\u2518   (A\u2081 is a subset of A)\n\nR1 = A\u2081        (40)\nR2 = A         (160)\nR3 = B         (160)   \u2190 same size as R2, completely different flares\nR4 = A \u222a B     (320)\n```\n\nAll four get **500 weight updates** and are scored on the **same 80 validation flares**. Three comparisons fall out:\n\n| comparison | question it answers |\n|---|---|\n| R1 \u2192 R2 \u2192 R4 (40 \u2192 160 \u2192 320) | *\"Runs with different amounts of training data, same validation.\"* Does more unique data actually help, or does 40 flares get you everything 320 does? |\n| **R2 vs R3** (160 vs a different 160) | *\"Runs with different training sets, same validation.\"* How much does the *choice* of flares matter? |\n| R0 vs all | *\"Baseline and finetuning runs with identical training and validation.\"* Does finetuning do anything at all? |\n\n**R2 vs R3 is the one that's easy to skip and shouldn't be.** It's your noise floor. Suppose R1 scores 0.21 and R2 scores 0.19 \u2014 is that \"more data helps\" or just luck in which flares landed in each set? You cannot tell. But if R2 and R3 (same size, different flares) differ by 0.03, then the 0.02 gap between R1 and R2 is noise and you'd be wrong to claim a trend. If R2 and R3 differ by 0.003, the same 0.02 gap is real.\n\nWithout R3 you have a scaling curve with no error bars, and the honest thing to say about it would be \"I can't distinguish this from chance.\" R3 is what lets you make a claim.\n\nThe nesting is why this costs 320 downloads instead of 680: R1's flares are inside R2's, and R4 is just A and B together, so nothing is downloaded twice."
    },
    {
      "raw_time": false,
      "time": 1789661016.3648233,
      "metadata": {
