          "settings": {},
          "id": null
        },
        "to_persona": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
        "settings": {
          "__mode__": "auto"
        }
      },
      "type": "msg",
      "id": "9a88914f-9a27-4ea8-b96b-f579cdfdfc14",
      "sender": "jpereira",
      "body": "ok i get why 4 runs. i also think i get why 4 workers: this means if i have more GB then more workers would make this faster, is that correct?"
    },
    {
      "type": "msg",
      "id": "88170e6e-c2b9-4cec-bdd5-48199f4d6e2b",
      "time": 1789661047.1937993,
      "body": "Correct, with a ceiling. RAM buys you workers, but workers buy you speed only until you run out of **CPU cores** \u2014 the 22.4 s is netCDF decompression, which is CPU work.\n\nYou have 8 physical cores (16 vCPU) and 71 GB free:\n\n| workers | RAM at `batch_size: 1` | s/sample | limited by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0 | ~5 GB | 22.4 | nothing overlaps \u2014 GPU idles |\n| 2 | ~16 GB | 11.2 | \u2014 |\n| **4** | **~30 GB** | **5.6** | my recommendation, safe margin |\n| 6 | ~45 GB | 3.7 | RAM getting tight |\n| 8 | ~60 GB | 2.8 | **both** limits hit at once |\n| 12 | ~90 GB | ~2.8, no gain | cores saturated; RAM wasted |\n\nYour two limits happen to coincide at 8 workers: that's simultaneously your 8 physical cores and your 60 GB ceiling. So on this machine 8 is the theoretical best, ~2.8 s/sample, and past that more RAM does nothing \u2014 the workers just take turns on the same cores and each one gets proportionally slower.\n\nSo the honest ladder is: 4 workers is the safe choice, 6 is a free 33% if RAM holds, 8 is the wall. Going from 4 to 8 would take the full plan from ~5.5 h to ~3 h \u2014 meaningful, but it's also exactly the configuration that killed your last run, just at `batch_size: 2` instead of 1. Test 6 during the dry run with `free -g` open before betting the night on it.\n\nOne thing that stays true at every setting: even at 2.8 s/sample you're still data-bound, because the GPU step is ~1-2 s. There is no worker count on this machine where the GPU becomes the bottleneck. That means the *structural* fix isn't more RAM \u2014 it's not re-reading and re-normalizing the same 320 flares 500 times a night. Caching the already-normalized tensors would attack the 22.4 s directly. I'd want to measure how much of that 22.4 s is disk versus CPU before promising a speedup, so it's a post-workshop item, not a tonight item.",
      "raw_time": false,
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "mentions": []
    },
