    {
      "id": "ca320152-fa01-4248-b7e8-b0ac2df4b5e5",
      "mentions": [],
      "raw_time": false,
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "time": 1789664177.9871202,
      "body": "Yes \u2014 change it to **`12m`**. Here's the evidence.\n\nThe gap between each flare's `peak_time` and the target frame it got matched to:\n\n| | min | 25% | median | 75% | 90% | 99% | max |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| train | 0 | 3 | **6** | 9 | 11 | 110 | **4,307** min (3 days) |\n| val | 0 | 3 | **6** | 9 | 11 | 316 | 1,526 min |\n\nThe distribution is bimodal and the break is sharp: **90% of samples sit within 11 minutes, then it jumps to hours and days.** There is nothing in between worth keeping.\n\n| tolerance | train kept | val kept | X-class | 2023 (test) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 6m | 3,501 (56%) | 189 (59%) | 28 | 64 |\n| **12m** | **6,026 (96%)** | **299 (93%)** | **38** | **97** |\n| 18m | 6,069 (97%) | 301 | 38 | 99 |\n| 1h | 6,183 | 309 | 39 | 101 |\n| 4d (current) | 6,279 | 321 | 39 | 107 |\n\n**Why 12m is the principled choice, not just a round number:** SDO's cadence is 12 minutes, and `ds_match_direction: forward` matches each Surya frame to the *next* flare peak. So a correctly matched sample can only land 0\u201311 minutes before the peak, uniformly. The median of 6 is exactly half of 12 \u2014 that's the fingerprint of a correct match. A gap larger than 12 minutes means there was no Surya frame near that flare at all, and the merge fell through to a later one. Those are junk by construction.\n\nSo `12m` costs you 253 train and 22 val samples that were never real matches, and keeps everything that was.\n\n**Don't go to 6m.** It looks tighter but it isn't better-matched \u2014 it just discards every flare that happened to fall in the second half of a 12-minute interval. You'd lose 44% of your data and gain nothing, because a 10-minute gap is exactly as legitimate as a 2-minute one.\n\nTwo consequences for the plan:\n\n- Your **test set shrinks from 107 to 97** samples, and val 2014\u201315 from 154 to 148. Both still fine; the val pool of 80 and the download budget are unaffected.\n- Worth saying in the talk: with `12m`, the frame the model is asked to predict sits **0\u201311 minutes before flare peak, median 6** \u2014 so it's predicting the rise phase, not the aftermath. That's the strongest single sentence you have for \"my `peak_time` anchoring works.\"\n\nOne reassurance on safety: tolerance is a pure post-hoc filter. It runs *after* `merge_asof` has already picked the nearest frame for each flare, so tightening it can only delete rows \u2014 it can never change which frame is paired with which flare. Nothing about your existing samples shifts.",
      "type": "msg"
    },
