    {
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "raw_time": false,
      "id": "8a8f9e95-28ee-40bf-86ce-b6a483dc8cb6",
      "type": "msg",
      "time": 1789664470.675832,
      "body": "## Average time from flare start to peak\n\n**Median 6 minutes. Mean 14 minutes.** Quote the median \u2014 the mean is inflated by catalog junk (the longest \"C-class rise\" in the file is 21,251 minutes, which is 15 days and obviously a bad record).\n\nBigger flares rise more slowly, as you'd expect:\n\n| GOES class | n | median rise | mean | 90th pct |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| C | 53,616 | **6 min** | 13.3 | 20 min |\n| M | 7,117 | **9 min** | 18.4 | 33 min |\n| X | 477 | **13 min** | 25.1 | 42 min |\n\nDecay (peak \u2192 end) is slower and separates the classes more sharply: C median 8 min, M 17 min, X 29 min.\n\n**The headline number: 76% of flares rise in less than one 12-minute SDO cadence step.** At SDO's sampling rate most flares are essentially instantaneous \u2014 they appear between one frame and the next.\n\n## What this means for your setup\n\nI ran the same arithmetic on your actual matched samples, so these are your frames, not the catalog at large:\n\n| | train (n=6,026) | val (n=299) |\n|---|---|---|\n| flare starts *after* the last input frame | **95.5%** | **98.0%** |\n| flare has already started at the target frame | 71.7% | 78.3% |\n| last input frame \u2192 peak | median 41 min | 41 min |\n| target frame \u2192 peak | median 5 min | 5 min |\n\nLaid out on a timeline, a typical sample looks like:\n\n```\n  -66 min        -42 min                      -6 min    0\n  input 1        input 2 (\"now\")              start   PEAK\n     |              |                           |      |\n     +--------------+---------------------------+------+\n      <-- model sees this -->      <-- predicts a frame in here -->\n                                        (target \u2248 peak - 5 min)\n```\n\nBoth input frames sit ~40 and ~65 minutes before the peak, and in **96% of your samples the flare has not started yet at the second input frame**. The target frame lands ~5 minutes before peak, by which point the flare is already underway in ~72% of samples.\n\nSo this is a genuine forecast, not an interpolation: the model is shown a quiet, pre-flare Sun and asked to draw a frame from partway up the flare's rise. That's the right framing for your presentation, and it also explains why the persistence baseline is so strong \u2014 copying the last input frame forward means copying a pre-flare Sun, which is correct for 99.94% of the pixels and wrong only in the flare footprint. It's also why `error_flare_region` matters: whole-image error can't distinguish \"predicted the flare\" from \"predicted quiet Sun.\"\n\nOne follow-on: your current `4d` tolerance lets in samples where the target frame is days from the flare. The table above is computed at `12m`, which keeps 6,026 train / 299 val and guarantees the \"target \u2248 peak \u2212 5 min\" relationship you'd be describing on a slide. At `4d` that claim isn't true for a few hundred samples.",
      "mentions": []
    },
    {
