    },
    {
      "id": "2bd99c7a-86d8-4e57-ac2d-c1c121c1271e",
      "time": 1789664985.8829398,
      "body": "No terminal needed \u2014 the measurements are in hand. One correction to your assumption first, because it drives the whole estimate: **the timing measurements are cached in this conversation, but the *data* is not.** Only 23 of your ~6,600 samples are fully cached on disk. The 2.8 TB already in `surya_cache` is from runs with different time offsets, so it doesn't help. Any 87 new flares means ~143 GB of fresh S3 downloads, and that download time is the single biggest term below.\n\n## Short answer\n\n**About 35 minutes if S3 downloads parallelize across workers, up to about 65 minutes if they don't.** So one real run with 87 flares costs roughly the same wall clock as the 4-run dry run did (56 min) \u2014 but it produces an actual result instead of just a stopwatch reading. Given you present tomorrow, that's the better trade.\n\n## What \"one run with 87 flares\" should be\n\n87 unique flares, split three ways. No flare appears in more than one group.\n\n| group | n | drawn from | purpose |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **train** | 55 | train split, spread across all available years (2010\u20132021) | the fine-tuning data |\n| **val** | 16 | val split, Jan 2014\u201315 (cycle 24 max) | the scored comparison |\n| **test** | 16 | val split, Jan 2023 (cycle 25 \u2014 real Hale reversal) | free bonus result |\n\nVal and test both come from the val split, but they're different years and different from train, so there's no leakage.\n\n## The run, in order\n\n| # | stage | samples read | cold or warm | why |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Baseline eval** \u2014 pretrained model, no training | 32 (val + test) | cold | This is your control. It also doubles as the plumbing check: if the prediction doesn't look like the Sun here, stop. |\n| 2 | **Train** epoch 1 | 55 | cold | |\n| 3 | **Train** epoch 2 | 55 | warm | Same 55 flares, now on local disk |\n| 4 | **Finetuned eval** \u2014 same 32 samples | 32 | warm | Directly comparable to stage 1 |\n\n**174 sample-reads total: 87 cold, 87 warm.** (The cold/warm split is why the number 87 shows up twice \u2014 every flare is paid for once at full price and once cheap.)\n\nIn stages 1 and 4, score **each sample twice** \u2014 once upright, once vertically flipped. That's two forward passes on one read, so it's nearly free, and it's the design that actually answers your question: same solar conditions in both orientations, so any difference is orientation and not sample luck.\n\n## Where the time goes\n\nMeasured costs: a cold sample read is ~63 s (about 40 s downloading 1.64 GB at ~41 MB/s, plus 22 s of CPU to decode and normalize); a warm read is ~22.4 s of pure CPU. The GPU step is ~2 s. **You are data-bound, not GPU-bound** \u2014 the GPU spends most of the run waiting.\n\n| | serial | \u00f7 4 workers |\n|---|---|---|\n| 87 cold reads | 91 min | 23 min |\n| 87 warm reads | 33 min | 8 min |\n| GPU (110 train steps + 128 forwards) | ~6 min | hidden behind the reads |\n| setup (build model, load weights, LoRA) | ~3 min | ~3 min |\n| **total** | | **~34 min** |\n\nThe uncertainty is entirely in the cold reads. The CPU half definitely splits across 4 workers (you have 8 physical cores). Whether the *download* half does depends on whether 41 MB/s is a per-connection limit or your total pipe \u2014 **I never got to measure that.** If it's the total pipe, 143 GB takes 58 minutes no matter how many workers you use, and the run lands nearer 65 min.\n\n**You'll know within 5 minutes.** Watch the per-sample time during stage 1:\n\n- **under ~20 s/sample** \u2192 downloads are parallelizing, you're on track for ~35 min\n- **~40 s/sample or worse** \u2192 bandwidth-capped, budget an hour\n\n## Settings\n\n| setting | value | why |\n|---|---|---|\n| `batch_size` | **1** | Each result row is then unambiguously flipped or not. Bigger batches buy nothing when you're data-bound. |\n| `num_workers` | **4** | 4 \u00d7 2 prefetch \u00d7 2.6 GB per sample \u2248 21 GB, ~26 GB with pinned memory. Well under your 60 GB ceiling. 6 workers would be ~40 GB \u2014 testable, but keep `free -g` open. |\n| epochs | **2** over 55 flares = 110 steps | Epoch 2 is nearly 3\u00d7 cheaper than epoch 1 since the cache is warm. |\n| `max_time` | `{\"minutes\": 45}` on the Trainer | Hard stop so a slow network can't eat your evening. |\n| validation during training | **off** | Stages 1 and 4 do all the scoring. Mid-training validation would just re-read samples. |\n| checkpoint monitoring | **off \u2014 use final weights** | With 110 steps there's no meaningful \"best\" epoch to select, and monitoring adds val passes. |\n| `flip_probability` | **0.5** for training | Random mixing is what you want in training. Evaluation uses the deliberate paired scoring instead. |\n| LoRA `target_modules` | `[fc1, fc2, attn.qkv, attn.proj]` | Currently 4 of the 6 names in your YAML are from a different model library and match nothing, so **attention is never adapted**. Fix it and the printed trainable-parameter count jumps \u2014 that's your confirmation. |\n\n## Two things that will bite you\n\n**1. `max_number_of_samples: 87` gives you the wrong 87.** It's a plain `[:87]` slice of a list sorted by flare peak time, so you'd get the 87 *earliest* flares \u2014 all June 2010, solar minimum, all C-class, one year. Your training set would have no variety at all. You need to pick the 55 by hand: build the dataset uncapped, then slice `valid_indices` and `df_valid_indices` down to a year-stratified index list (roughly 6 per available year). That's a few lines in a notebook cell, no new files.\n\n**2. Draw your dry-run samples as a subset of whatever larger pools you'd use later.** If this run goes well and you want the full 4-run study overnight, having chosen these 87 as a subset means you never re-download them.\n\n## What you get, and what you give up\n\n**You get** two of your advisor's three experiments:\n\n- **Baseline vs. fine-tuned on identical data** \u2014 stage 1 vs. stage 4, which was explicitly on the list.\n- **Flipped vs. upright** \u2014 your actual hypothesis, on both the baseline and the fine-tuned model.\n- **Cycle 24 vs. cycle 25 for real** \u2014 the 2014\u201315 rows vs. the 2023 rows. This one is free and it's the strongest thing on your slide, because it's real data rather than a simulated flip. If the flip result and the real-cycle result agree, that's a much better finding than either alone.\n- **A calibrated timing model.** The measured seconds-per-sample from this run tells you exactly what the full 4-run study would cost, so you don't lose the dry run's purpose.\n\n**You give up** the data-scaling curve and the ensemble \u2014 those need multiple runs by construction. And be honest with yourself about magnitude: 110 LoRA steps will not transform a 366M-parameter model. Expect a small change in the numbers. The value here is the comparison structure, not the size of the effect, and that's a perfectly reasonable thing to say in a workshop talk.\n\nOne thing to keep next to every error number you report: **the persistence baseline** (just copy the last input frame forward). You're forecasting a frame ~40 minutes ahead of a Sun that's still quiet in 96% of your samples, so copying is a genuinely strong guess. If your model doesn't beat it, say so plainly \u2014 that's a finding, not a failure.",
      "type": "msg",
      "mentions": [],
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "raw_time": false
    },
