      "body": "First \u2014 what I was doing and why, since I should have said so up front: I ran **read-only** inspections. I dumped the cell list and contents of `2c_flip_minimal.ipynb` (21 cells) so my instructions would point at the right cell numbers, and I read `workshop_infrastructure/datasets/builders.py` to see how the DataLoaders get built so the replacement code matches. **Nothing was written or modified.** From here on I'll tell you before running anything.\n\n---\n\n## Why `max_number_of_samples` gives you the earliest flares\n\nIn `datasets/flare_dataset.py:119-122` the cap is a plain slice:\n\n```python\nself.valid_indices = self.valid_indices[:max_number_of_samples]\n```\n\nand that list was sorted by flare peak time a few lines earlier. So `max_number_of_samples=87` means **the 87 chronologically first flares** \u2014 all June 2010, solar minimum, all C-class. There's no shuffle anywhere in that path.\n\n**The fix:** build the datasets *uncapped*, then replace three attributes with a randomly-chosen subset. Those three are the only things that define which flares a dataset serves:\n\n| attribute | used by |\n|---|---|\n| `valid_indices` | the base class, to pick which timestamps to load |\n| `df_valid_indices` | `flare_Dataset.__getitem__`, for the label and metadata |\n| `adjusted_length` | `__len__` |\n\nSlice all three with the same position list and the dataset is a genuine random subset. I also stratify by year (round-robin across years, random within each year) so you don't get 55 flares from one year by luck.\n\n---\n\n## The changes, in one table\n\n| cell | action | why |\n|---|---|---|\n| **4** | **replace entirely** | random stratified subsets; 3 loaders (train/val/test); `batch_size=1`; 4 workers |\n| **11** | **two small edits** | it currently loops over *every* sample \u2014 71 cold reads \u2248 75 min. Cap it at 1. |\n| **new, after 11** | **add** | define `evaluate()` |\n| **new, after that** | **add** | Stage 1: baseline pass on the pretrained model |\n| **12** | **replace the Trainer block** | `max_time` guard, no validation during fit, no checkpoint monitor |\n| **13** | **replace** | fit on the train loader only |\n| **new, after 15** | **add** | Stage 4: finetuned pass + the comparison tables |\n| `configs/config_script.yaml` line 148 | **one-line edit** | LoRA currently never touches attention |\n\nCells 5\u201310, 14, 15 and 17\u201319 need no changes. I deliberately kept the names `train_data_loader` / `val_data_loader` so cells 11, 14 and 15 keep working.\n\n---\n\n## Cell 4 \u2014 replace the whole cell\n\n```python\n# ============================== knobs ========================================\nFLIP_PROBABILITY = 0.5        # training mix: 0.0 upright | 0.5 mixed | 1.0 flipped\nN_TRAIN, N_VAL, N_TEST = 55, 16, 16       # 87 unique flares in total\nVAL_YEARS  = (2014, 2015)     # cycle 24 maximum\nTEST_YEARS = (2023,)          # cycle 25 -- the real polarity reversal, touched once\nSUBSET_SEED    = 42           # change this to draw a DIFFERENT random training set\nLOADER_WORKERS = 4            # host RAM while a loader runs ~ workers x PREFETCH x 2.6 GB\nPREFETCH       = 2            # drop to 1 if `free -g` climbs toward 60\n# =============================================================================\nimport copy, random\nfrom torch.utils.data import DataLoader\nfrom downstream_apps.flare_forecast.datasets.flare_dataset import flare_Dataset\nfrom workshop_infrastructure.datasets.builders import build_helio_datasets\n\n# 1. Build the FULL sets. Index matching only -- no NetCDF is read, so this is fast.\ntrain_full, val_full = build_helio_datasets(\n    cfg, flare_Dataset, scalers=scalers,\n    return_surya_stack=True,\n    max_number_of_samples=None,          # <-- uncapped: no [:N] earliest slice\n    flip_probability=0.0,\n    ds_flare_index_path=cfg.data.flare_index_path,\n    ds_time_column=cfg.data.ds_time_column,\n    ds_time_tolerance=cfg.data.ds_time_tolerance,\n    ds_match_direction=cfg.data.ds_match_direction,\n)\n# val and test are different YEARS of the same split, so test starts as a shallow copy.\n# take() rebinds attributes rather than mutating them, so the two stay independent.\ntest_full = copy.copy(val_full)\n\n\n# 2. Pick a RANDOM subset, spread as evenly as possible across the available years.\ndef pick_stratified(ds, n, years=None, seed=0):\n    times = pd.DatetimeIndex(ds.valid_indices)\n    pool = np.arange(len(times))\n    if years is not None:\n        pool = pool[np.isin(times.year, list(years))]\n    if len(pool) < n:\n        raise ValueError(f\"only {len(pool)} samples available for years={years}, need {n}\")\n    rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n    buckets = {}\n    for p in pool:\n        buckets.setdefault(times[p].year, []).append(p)\n    for y in buckets:\n        rng.shuffle(buckets[y])                  # random within each year\n    picked = []\n    while len(picked) < n:                       # round-robin across years\n        for y in sorted(buckets):\n            if buckets[y] and len(picked) < n:\n                picked.append(buckets[y].pop())\n    return sorted(picked)\n\n\ndef take(ds, positions):\n    \"\"\"Shrink a dataset to `positions`. These three attributes ARE the sample list.\"\"\"\n    ds.valid_indices    = [ds.valid_indices[i] for i in positions]\n    ds.df_valid_indices = ds.df_valid_indices.iloc[positions]\n    ds.adjusted_length  = len(positions)\n    return ds\n\n\ntrain_ds = take(train_full, pick_stratified(train_full, N_TRAIN, seed=SUBSET_SEED))\nval_ds   = take(val_full,   pick_stratified(val_full,  N_VAL,  years=VAL_YEARS,  seed=SUBSET_SEED))\ntest_ds  = take(test_full,  pick_stratified(test_full, N_TEST, years=TEST_YEARS, seed=SUBSET_SEED + 1))\n\ntrain_ds.flip_probability = FLIP_PROBABILITY\nval_ds.flip_probability = test_ds.flip_probability = 0.0   # evaluate() flips these by hand\n\n\n# 3. Loaders. batch_size=1 so every result row is unambiguously flipped or not.\ndef _init_worker(worker_id):\n    s = (SUBSET_SEED + worker_id) % 2**32\n    random.seed(s); np.random.seed(s); torch.manual_seed(s)\n\ndef make_loader(ds, shuffle):\n    kw = dict(batch_size=1, num_workers=LOADER_WORKERS, pin_memory=True, drop_last=False)\n    if LOADER_WORKERS > 0:\n        kw.update(\n            multiprocessing_context=\"spawn\",   # the boto3 handle does not survive fork\n            persistent_workers=False,          # workers die at epoch end, so RAM is released\n            prefetch_factor=PREFETCH,\n            worker_init_fn=_init_worker,\n        )\n    g = torch.Generator(); g.manual_seed(SUBSET_SEED)\n    return DataLoader(ds, shuffle=shuffle, generator=(g if shuffle else None), **kw)\n\ntrain_data_loader = make_loader(train_ds, shuffle=True)\nval_data_loader   = make_loader(val_ds,   shuffle=False)\ntest_data_loader  = make_loader(test_ds,  shuffle=False)\n\nbatch_size = 1            # cell 10 reads this\nN_SAMPLES  = N_TRAIN      # the \"extra checks\" cells at the bottom still refer to this name\n\nfor _n, _d in ((\"train\", train_ds), (\"val\", val_ds), (\"test\", test_ds)):\n    _t = pd.DatetimeIndex(_d.valid_indices)\n    _c = _d.df_valid_indices[\"GOES_class\"].str[0].value_counts().to_dict()\n    print(f\"{_n:5s} {len(_d):3d} flares | years {dict(sorted(_t.year.value_counts().items()))} \"\n          f\"| class {dict(sorted(_c.items()))} | flip_p {_d.flip_probability}\")\nprint(f\"\\n{len(train_ds) + len(val_ds) + len(test_ds)} unique flares | batch_size {batch_size} | \"\n      f\"{LOADER_WORKERS} workers | ~{LOADER_WORKERS * PREFETCH * 2.6:.0f} GB host RAM per active loader\")\n```\n\n**Check the printout.** The `years` dict for train should show several years with a handful each \u2014 if it shows only `{2010: 55}` the stratification didn't take.\n\n---\n\n## Cell 11 \u2014 two edits, or it eats 75 minutes\n\nAs written, part B loops `for i in range(len(d))` over both splits. That was fine at 6 samples; at 55 + 16 it's 71 single-process cold reads. Change **one line**:\n\n```python\n    for i in range(len(d)):\n```\nto\n```python\n    for i in range(1):        # one sample is enough -- evaluate() scores the whole set properly\n```\n\nAnd at the bottom, this line hands `val` the *training* flip probability, which would double-flip during evaluation:\n\n```python\nfor d in (train_data_loader.dataset, val_data_loader.dataset):\n    d.flip_probability = saved_p\n```\nchange to\n```python\ntrain_data_loader.dataset.flip_probability = saved_p   # val/test must stay at 0.0\n```\n\n(`evaluate()` also forces `0.0` defensively, so this is belt-and-braces.)\n\n---\n\n## New cell after 11 \u2014 define `evaluate()`\n\n```python\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Paired evaluation: each sample is READ ONCE and SCORED TWICE, upright and\n# vertically flipped. Both orientations therefore contain identical solar\n# conditions, so any difference is orientation and not sample luck.\n#\n#   error               whole-image MSE in normalized space (1.0 = \"predicted the average\")\n#   error_persistence   MSE of just copying the last input frame forward. THE bar to beat.\n#   error_flare_region  MSE over the brightest 0.1% pixels of the true frame only\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nimport time\n\nEVAL_KEYS = (\"ts\", \"time_delta_input\", \"lead_time_delta\")     # all the backbone reads\nMASK_CHAN = \"aia131\" if \"aia131\" in cfg.data.channels else cfg.data.channels[0]\nMASK_FRAC = 0.001\n\ndef _mse(a, b):\n    return torch.mean((a.float() - b.float()) ** 2).item()\n\n@torch.no_grad()\ndef evaluate(lit, tag, loaders):\n    dev = \"cuda\" if torch.cuda.is_available() else \"cpu\"\n    lit = lit.to(dev).eval()\n    ci = list(cfg.data.channels).index(MASK_CHAN)\n    rows, t0 = [], time.perf_counter()\n\n    for split, dl in loaders.items():\n        d = dl.dataset\n        d.flip_probability = 0.0      # we flip by hand; a random flip here would double-flip\n        for i, batch in enumerate(dl):            # shuffle=False, so batch i == dataset row i\n            meta = d.df_valid_indices.iloc[i]\n            up  = {k: batch[k].to(dev) for k in EVAL_KEYS}\n            tgt = batch[\"forecast\"][:, :, 0].to(dev).float()      # (1, 13, 4096, 4096)\n\n            # \"the flaring region\": brightest pixels of the ground truth in one AIA channel.\n            # Whole-image error is 99.9% quiet Sun, so it cannot see whether the flare landed.\n            flat = tgt[0, ci].reshape(-1)\n            thr  = torch.topk(flat, max(1, int(MASK_FRAC * flat.numel()))).values[-1]\n            mask = tgt[0, ci] >= thr\n\n            for orient in (\"upright\", \"flipped\"):\n                b, t, m = up, tgt, mask\n                if orient == \"flipped\":\n                    b = {**up, \"ts\": torch.flip(up[\"ts\"], dims=[-2])}\n                    t = torch.flip(tgt, dims=[-2])\n                    m = torch.flip(mask, dims=[-2])\n                with torch.autocast(device_type=dev, dtype=torch.bfloat16):\n                    pred = lit(b).float()\n                rows.append(dict(\n                    run=tag, split=split, i=i, orientation=orient,\n                    was_flipped=(orient == \"flipped\"),\n                    time=str(d.valid_indices[i])[:16],\n                    year=d.valid_indices[i].year,\n                    GOES_class=meta[\"GOES_class\"],\n                    error=_mse(pred, t),\n                    error_persistence=_mse(b[\"ts\"][:, :, -1], t),\n                    error_flare_region=_mse(pred[:, :, m], t[:, :, m]),\n                ))\n                del pred, b, t, m\n            del up, tgt, mask, flat, batch\n            if dev == \"cuda\":\n                torch.cuda.empty_cache()\n            el = time.perf_counter() - t0\n            print(f\"\\r  [{tag}] {split} {i+1}/{len(d)}   {el/(len(rows)/2):5.1f} s/sample   \"\n                  f\"elapsed {el/60:4.1f} min\", end=\"\", flush=True)\n    print()\n\n    df = pd.DataFrame(rows)\n    # Persistence never touches the model, so it MUST match across orientations.\n    _p = df.pivot_table(index=[\"split\", \"i\"], columns=\"orientation\", values=\"error_persistence\")\n    gap = (_p[\"upright\"] - _p[\"flipped\"]).abs().max()\n    assert gap < 1e-4, f\"persistence differs by {gap:.2e} across orientations -- flip inconsistent\"\n    print(f\"  persistence gap across orientations {gap:.1e}  (must be ~0)\")\n\n    lit.to(\"cpu\")\n    if dev == \"cuda\":\n        torch.cuda.empty_cache()\n    return df\n\nprint(f\"evaluate() ready. flare-region mask from {MASK_CHAN}, top {MASK_FRAC:.1%} of pixels.\")\n```\n\n## New cell after that \u2014 Stage 1, the baseline\n\nKeep this separate from the definition so you can re-run the definition without repeating a 20-minute pass.\n\n```python\n# STAGE 1: the pretrained model with no fine-tuning. LoRA adapters start at zero\n# contribution, so this really is stock Surya -- it is your control.\n# Every sample here is a cold S3 read, so this is the slowest stage. It also warms\n# the cache for the finetuned pass at the end.\ndf_baseline = evaluate(lit_model, \"baseline\", {\"val\": val_data_loader, \"test\": test_data_loader})\ndf_baseline.to_csv(\"results_baseline.csv\", index=False)\nprint(df_baseline.groupby([\"split\", \"orientation\"])[\n    [\"error\", \"error_persistence\", \"error_flare_region\"]].mean().to_string())\n```\n\n---\n\n## Cell 12 \u2014 replace the Trainer block\n\nKeep your `wandb_logger` / `csv_logger` lines as they are; swap `max_epochs = 2` and the `trainer = ...` block for:\n\n```python\nMAX_EPOCHS = 2      # epoch 1 downloads; epoch 2 reads from cache and is ~3x faster\n\ntrainer = L.Trainer(\n    max_epochs=MAX_EPOCHS,\n    max_time={\"minutes\": 45},        # hard wall-clock stop so a slow network can't eat the evening\n    accelerator=\"auto\",\n    devices=\"auto\",\n    precision=\"bf16-mixed\",\n    logger=[wandb_logger, csv_logger],\n    # No validation during fit -- the two evaluate() passes do all the scoring, and a\n    # mid-training val pass would re-read samples for nothing.\n    limit_val_batches=0,\n    # No monitor: at ~110 steps there is no meaningful \"best\" epoch, so keep the final\n    # weights. save_last still writes last.ckpt in case the kernel dies.\n    callbacks=[ModelCheckpoint(save_top_k=0, save_last=True)],\n    log_every_n_steps=1,\n)\nprint(f\"{MAX_EPOCHS} epochs x {len(train_data_loader)} steps = \"\n      f\"{MAX_EPOCHS * len(train_data_loader)} optimizer steps\")\n```\n\n## Cell 13 \u2014 replace\n\n```python\n# Training mix goes on now. Only the TRAINING set flips randomly; val and test stay at\n# 0.0 because evaluate() flips them by hand to score each sample in both orientations.\ntrain_data_loader.dataset.flip_probability = FLIP_PROBABILITY\nval_data_loader.dataset.flip_probability = 0.0\ntest_data_loader.dataset.flip_probability = 0.0\n\ntrainer.fit(lit_model, train_data_loader)      # no val loader -> no validation passes\n```\n\n## New cell after 15 \u2014 Stage 4 and the results\n\n```python\n# STAGE 4: the same 32 samples, same paired scoring, now with fine-tuned weights.\n# All warm reads, so this runs about 3x faster than the baseline pass.\ndf_finetuned = evaluate(lit_model, \"finetuned\", {\"val\": val_data_loader, \"test\": test_data_loader})\ndf_finetuned.to_csv(\"results_finetuned.csv\", index=False)\n\nres = pd.concat([df_baseline, df_finetuned], ignore_index=True)\nres.to_csv(\"results_all.csv\", index=False)\ncols = [\"error\", \"error_persistence\", \"error_flare_region\"]\n\nprint(\"\\n=== 1. does orientation matter? (your hypothesis) ===\")\nprint(res.groupby([\"run\", \"orientation\"])[cols].mean().to_string())\n\nprint(\"\\n=== 2. cycle 24 (val, 2014-15) vs cycle 25 (test, 2023) -- the real thing ===\")\nprint(res.groupby([\"run\", \"split\", \"orientation\"])[cols].mean().to_string())\n\nprint(\"\\n=== 3. did fine-tuning help at all? ===\")\nprint(res.groupby(\"run\")[cols].mean().to_string())\n\n# The headline number. Positive means flipping makes the model worse.\npiv = res.pivot_table(index=[\"run\", \"split\", \"i\"], columns=\"orientation\", values=\"error\")\nprint(\"\\nflipped minus upright, per sample (positive = flipping hurts):\")\nprint((piv[\"flipped\"] - piv[\"upright\"]).groupby(level=[\"run\", \"split\"])\n      .agg([\"mean\", \"std\", \"count\"]).to_string())\n```\n\n---\n\n## One config edit\n\n`configs/config_script.yaml` line 148:\n\n```yaml\ntarget_modules: [q_proj, v_proj, k_proj, out_proj, fc1, fc2]\n```\n\u2192\n```yaml\ntarget_modules: [fc1, fc2, attn.qkv, attn.proj]\n```\n\nFour of those six names come from a different model library and match nothing in Surya. PEFT only errors when *nothing* matches, and `fc1`/`fc2` do, so it runs happily while **never adapting a single attention layer**. After the change, cell 7's trainable-parameter count should go up \u2014 that's your confirmation.\n\n(Optional tidy: cell 1 still sets `WANDB_NOTEBOOK_NAME = \"2b_flip_minimal.ipynb\"`.)\n\n---\n\n## What it should cost\n\n| stage | reads | cold/warm |\n|---|---|---|\n| cell 11 checks | ~4 | 2 cold, single-process |\n| Stage 1 baseline | 32 | ~31 cold |\n| fit epoch 1 | 55 | ~54 cold |\n| fit epoch 2 | 55 | warm |\n| cells 14\u201315 | ~3 | warm |\n| Stage 4 finetuned | 32 | warm |\n| **total** | **~181** | **87 cold, ~94 warm** |\n\nCold read \u2248 63 s (40 s downloading 1.64 GB + 22 s CPU decode/normalize); warm \u2248 22 s of pure CPU. Divided across 4 workers, plus ~3 min of model setup and ~3 min of single-process checks:\n\n**\u2248 37 minutes if S3 downloads parallelize across workers; \u2248 65\u201370 minutes if they don't.** You'll download about 143 GB into `surya_cache`. I never got to measure whether that 41 MB/s is per-connection or your whole pipe, which is the entire spread in that estimate.\n\n**The decisive number shows up 5 minutes in.** Watch the `s/sample` counter that `evaluate()` prints during Stage 1:\n\n- **under ~20 s/sample** \u2192 downloads are parallelizing, you're on track for ~37 min\n- **~40 s/sample or worse** \u2192 bandwidth-capped; budget an hour, and consider dropping `N_TRAIN` to 40\n\nAlso keep a terminal on `free -g`. Expected peak is ~26 GB, well under your 60 GB ceiling; if it climbs, set `PREFETCH = 1`.\n\n**Run order:** cells 1 \u2192 15 in sequence, then the new final cell. Two honest caveats: 110 LoRA steps will not transform a 366M-parameter model, so expect small movements \u2014 the value is the comparison structure. And every error number needs `error_persistence` beside it, because you're predicting a frame ~40 min ahead of a Sun that's still quiet in 96% of these samples, so copying the last frame is a genuinely strong guess.",
      "type": "msg",
      "id": "be78e52d-ceea-4cb7-868a-0ba9e6a67917",
